\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Japanese PM's comments drive yen higher. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2001\par 09:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 3 (Reuters) - The yen rose around a quarter percent on Tuesday against both the dollar and the euro after Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi said he had no intention of deliberately driving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. \par \par \uc2  The comments in an interview with the Financial Times were the new prime minister's first substantive comment on yen policy and helped drive the dollar down more than a yen from Monday's 2-1/2-month highs near 125 yen. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the comments coming so soon after Koizumi's weekend summit with President George W. Bush seemed to put the final nail in the coffin of any thoughts about a joint U.S.-Japan agreement on lowering the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The Koizumi statement is one of the first explicit comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from him we've had and it's got people wary even of possible BOJ (Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," to stop the yen falling too rapidly said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economic adviser at the Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  The euro also fell against the yen, trading around 104.81 yen at 0730 GMT from 105.15 in New York. But it was stuck in a fairly tight range around $0.8465. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there was little appetite for trading ahead of the U.S. holiday on Wednesday and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate-setting meeting on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  ECB VIGIL \par \par \uc2  Analysts said expectations were growing that the ECB would cut rates by a quarter point when it concludes its meeting on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  They said the deterioration in French consumer confidence in June highlighted the fragile state of the euro zone economy, coming as it did only a day after a report signalled euro zone manufacturing activity contracting at a faster pace. \par \par \uc2  The French Insee consumer confidence index dropped to -8 in June from -6 in the previous month (revised from -7). \par \par \uc2  "The French economy's meant to be the strong one in the euro zone and even there things aren't going so well," said a trader at a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  "Thoughts of an ECB cut are a small positive for the euro at the moment," he added. \par \par \uc2  ECB President Wim Duisenberg is speaking before the European parliament later in the day and dealers were hoping he might be a little less ambiguous on policy than in recent remarks. \par \par \uc2  Also due are consumer and business confidence figures from the European Commission but these are expected to be just as soft as the purchasing management surveys were on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The euro also found some psychological supported against the yen on talk that the launch on Monday of a 155 billion yen ($1.25 billion) bond by France Telecom could induce euro purchases worth 50-70 billion yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  STRONG DOLLAR SYNDROME \par \par \uc2  Dollar bulls were unhappy to hear fresh squeals of pain about the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from U.S. industry, with the latest plaintiff being the influential National Association of Purchasing Management (NAPM). \par \par \uc2  While the NAPM survey for June showed some stabilisation in the hard-hit industrial sector, the head of its business survey committee Norbert Ore voiced concern that the strong dollar was making it very tough on exporters. \par \par \uc2  The NAPM joins the National Association of Manufacturers in complaining about the dollar, which just last week hit a 15-year high in trade weighted terms. \par \par \uc2  Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill has repeatedly rejected such complaints. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers were trying to between the lines of a comment from Robert Zoellick, the administration's top trade negotiator. \par \par \uc2  In a Wall Street Journal article he suggested that if Japan's restructuring efforts showed some success it could lead to fund flows into Japan and so into the yen. \par \par \uc2  Analysts noted, however, that the Japanese themselves had warned it could take two to three years for reforms to be felt, making Zoellick's scenario one for the long term. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010912dx7300dg3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
