\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar fails to capitalise on weak yen endorsement. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 661 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2001\par 05:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 21 (Reuters) - The dollar was well supported but failed to break through key resistance of 124 yen on Thursday morning even after Washington showed understanding to a possible fall in the yen if it resulted from Japan's carrying out reforms. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 123.85/90 yen more than a half yen higher than a day ago, but only slightly firmer than 123.74 yen in late U.S. trade. The dollar began receiving tail wind in late U.S. trade after a senior economic adviser for the Bush administration was reported to have said he did not see problems in a weaker yen as a result of structural reforms in Japan. \par \par \uc2  Lawrence Lindsey's comments as reported in Japanese economic daily Nihon Keizai Shimbun pushed the dollar as high as 124.06 yen in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  "His comments confirmed that the U.S. has not abandoned the strong dollar policy," said Shingo Funatsuki, manager at Citibank N.A. in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Funatsuki also said Japanese Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka had been making similar comments. \par \par \uc2  "It's interesting that key people in both countries are in accord that Japan's structural reform would lead to a weaker yen," said Funatsuki. \par \par \uc2  But market players were careful not to over-react to Lindsey's remark, since he qualified his view to say that intentionally guiding the yen lower to solve the economy's woes was not acceptable. \par \par \uc2  That view disappointed some dollar bulls, who had hoped for such an aggressive policy, possibly through further monetary easing by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ), or even direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the bank to buy the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Japanese exporters blocked the dollar's breach of 124 yen in Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  The dollar made a wild gyration late in the morning when Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa was quoted as saying his ministry was ready to act in the event of any big moves in the exchange market. \par \par \uc2  Almost simultaneously, White House spokeswoman Jeanie Mamo repeated Lindsey's statement on the yen rate. \par \par \uc2  The White House's rare reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to lift the greenback, making dollar bulls increasingly nervous. \par \par \uc2  "For some reason, people were very conscious of the 124.06 yen level. If the dollar fails to break through that area again, there will be some adjustment," said a dealer at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, some dealers said they were starting to believe the dollar would not have the strength to rise, particularly to above 125 yen, in the near future. \par \par \uc2  "Structural reforms have yet to begin. That means a weaker yen is not okay (for Washington) now," said Takashi Yamanaka, economist at Sanwa Bank. \par \par \uc2  The government is expected to unveil its blueprint for reforms in the afternoon, but analysts said it would take months to get it through parliament. \par \par \uc2  Still, many dealers see the yen staying under pressure, given the dismal picture for the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  In another confirmation on that, the government said in the morning that economic activity in April fell down 2.5 percent from the previous month. More worrying was the 3.6 percent fall in the tertiary sector index, which some consider to be a close proxy of GDP. It was the largest fall for both indices since April 1997. \par \par \uc2  Sanwa's Yamanaka said the figures suggest it would be very difficult for Japan to grow in the April-June quarter. \par \par \uc2  The euro was firmer at 85.53/58 cents compared with 85.44 cents in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, it also gained a little to 105.80/91 yen against 105.69 yen at the end of Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "People were closing their euro short positions ahead of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (ECB) council meeting today. Though many think the ECB will not do anything today, it's better to play it safe," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6l0193s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
