\par\par \b \uc2  Yen holds out despite dwindling trade surplus. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Isabel Reynolds \par \uc2 762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2001\par 04:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 20 (Reuters) - The dollar inched up against the yen in Wednesday morning trade on data showing a sharp drop in Japan's trade surplus in May, but gains were tempered due to yen buying by Japanese exporters, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The greenback was trading around 122.96 up from the previous day's low of 122.67, but still off the recent four-week high of 123.75. \par \par \uc2  Weighing on yen sentiment was an unpalatable set of data released in the morning, showing Japan's trade surplus fell by 86.1 percent in May from a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained some support through the morning from exporters' sales of euros at around 105 yen, but dealers said such transactions may shrink dramatically if the trade surplus continues to fall. \par \par \uc2  The effect of the trade figures on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels was relatively slight, however, given the dramatic headline numbers. \par \par \uc2  "I think the fall in the trade surplus was within expectations," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  "People are not so concerned about today's figure because the trend has been around for some time," he explained, though he said it was undeniably a yen-negative factor. \par \par \uc2  Growing concerns about the economy were also brought into focus by a report in the Mainichi Shimbun saying the government is planning to downgrade its economic growth outlook for this fiscal to zero from the current 1.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  Besides yen-buying by exporters, some dealers said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buoyed by a Tuesday evening news conference by Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami, who said a weaker yen would be detrimental for the global economy and could ultimately backfire on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  He said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be necessary if the market moved too quickly. \par \par \uc2  HAYAMI EFFECT TEMPORARY \par \par \uc2  Although analysts said they agree with Hayami, many say it will be hard for him to put the brakes on the trend toward a weaker yen in the longer term, pointing out that it is actually the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  that controls \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  "What he said was sensible... but I don't think the market is going to move much on the basis of those comments. We are expecting a move toward a cheaper yen," said Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi's Fukaya. \par \par \uc2  The euro traded sideways against the dollar and was bid at $0.8528 compared with $0.8537 in late New York, where it had been squeezed by a warning from German Economics Minister Werner Mueller that GDP growth could fall to zero in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  made some small gains against the yen after the Japanese trade figures, but gave them up later to stand at around 104.89 compared with 104.82 in late New York. \par \par \uc2  Market players are now focusing on a speech by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan due to be delivered in Washington at 1245 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Dealers are looking for hints as to the size of a widely expected cut in interest rates at the Fed's policy-setting meeting on June 26 and 27. But few believe the veteran central banker will say anything to influence the markets inn advance. \par \par \uc2  "The questions people are turning to now are whether there will be another inter-meeting rate cut and whether there will be another cut in August," said Fukaya. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0213 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2000 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab      19 Jun.\par \tab \tab \tab   -----------------------------------------\par   Euro/dlr\tab \tab   0.8527   0.8537   -0.12   -10.20   0.9496\par   Dlr/yen\tab \tab    123.00   122.79   +0.17    +7.47   114.45\par   Euro/yen\tab \tab   104.86   104.82   +0.04    -3.54   108.71\par   Dlr/swiss\tab \tab  1.7906   1.7893   +0.07   +11.83   1.6012\par   Stg/dlr\tab \tab    1.3986   1.4013   -0.19    -6.76   1.5000\par   Can/dlr\tab \tab    1.5339   1.5337   +0.01    +2.85   1.4914\par   Aus/dlr\tab \tab    0.5182   0.5202   -0.38    -7.37   0.5594\par   Euro/swiss\tab \tab 1.5270   1.5281   -0.07    +0.32   1.5221\par   Euro/stg\tab \tab   0.6093   0.6089   +0.07    -3.67   0.6325.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par .. Debt... Indicators... \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports.. All spots Tokyo spots Europe spots Volatilities Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Official rates...\tab \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Diary.......\tab \tab  Top\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  events........\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Diaries........... Diaries Index........ Press Digests.... Polls on G7 economies.. Reuters Television..\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6k014jv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
