\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Hayami: Wrong To Forcibly Weaken Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 756 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 2001\par 09:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The Bank of Japan isn't thinking of loosening its ultra-easy monetary policy further, given that liquidity is already at adequate levels and the real economic benefit of further easing is questionable, the governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  While noting that the recent decline in exports is weighing on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy, Masaru Hayami said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen is undesirable and that the nation doesn't necessarily need a cheaper \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it is running a large current account surplus. \par \par \uc2  "There has been a lot of speculation in the media that the BOJ may take an extra quantitative easing step. As there is already ample liquidity in the money market, we're not thinking of that now," Hayami told a regular press conference. \par \par \uc2  "Just like giving a plant too much water doesn't help it to grow, it is questionable whether easing again would have real economic benefits," he said. \par \par \uc2  Some economists have called for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the yen in order to stave off deflation. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is the domain of the Finance Ministry and not the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  "I think a forcible action to weaken the yen is wrong," said Hayami. "A lower yen might help Japan's exports, but Japan's current account balance is in surplus, while the U.S.'s is in deficit, so there's no real need for a weaker yen." \par \par \uc2  The governor was speaking after the BOJ policy board voted unanimously last Friday to leave unchanged its current policy, implemented in March, of guiding bank-reserves to Y5 trillion. The move sent short-term interest rates to virtually zero. \par \par \uc2  The independent BOJ stuck with this policy despite calls from senior government officials to ease amid signs that the struggling economy is falling into recession. Data released last week showed that economic growth contracted by an annualized 0.8% in the January-March quarter, while other indicators have shown that deflation remains in a severe state. \par \par \uc2  Hayami's resistance to loosening policy at Tuesday's press conference wasn't surprising. \par \par \uc2  Appearing in Parliament earlier in the day, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor was quick to rule out the need for further monetary stimulus despite being grilled by politicians demanding to know why the BOJ policy board had opted to leave policy unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Hayami said the BOJ is in no hurry to ease its grip on credit, as current economic conditions don't warrant it. \par \par \uc2  He added at the press conference that the BOJ had undertaken the experimental March quantitative easing on the expectation that the economy would deteriorate further and that the recent signs of contraction are within its expectations. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ governor, however, didn't deny the need for further easing altogether. \par \par \uc2  Repeating a long-held BOJ view, Hayami said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prepared to soften monetary conditions more if painful government-promised reforms, such as getting banks to write off bad loans, have a nasty deflationary impact. \par \par \uc2  The governor also said that a sharp downturn in the world economy or a further IT-related shock in financial markets, for example, could presage further action. \par \par \uc2  However, Hayami noted that government progress on reform is currently slow due to the approaching Upper House elections in July, and he urged the private sector to take a more active role in restructuring the economy back to health. \par \par \uc2  Hayami's ongoing reluctance to heed calls for further easing dampen suspicion that the BOJ may have secretly agreed with the government to exercise an option to buy more Japanese government bonds outright - which is the same as printing more money. \par \par \uc2  "There's no need to increase JGB purchases now," he said. \par \par \uc2  Hayami added that Economy Minister Heizo Takenaka's insistence on attending the second day of the BOJ policy board meeting last Friday as an observer was "not strange," as economic ministers had done that in the past. \par \par \uc2  "Although I can't reveal the details, we appreciated his explanation of the government's structural reform plans," Hayami said. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ governor was speaking the day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  downgraded its view of the economy in its monthly report for June, saying that adjustments in economic activity are "gradually intensifying" amid a continuing slide in exports. \par \par \uc2  The government also downgraded its economic assessment last week, noting that "the economy is deteriorating". \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Nick Shindo and Yukiko Tsuba, Dow Jones Newswires;\par 813-5255-2929; nick.shindo@dowjones.com; yukiko.tsuba@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx6j02s16\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
