\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 1-Asian equities supported by bounce in New York. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Cooper \par \uc2 692 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2001\par 09:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 13 (Reuters) - Asian share markets were flat to slightly higher on Wednesday, underpinned by relief tied to a dramatic late session recovery in U.S. stocks on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Nikkei 225 Index gave up early gains to end the day down 16.65 points or 0.13 percent at 12,823.45. \par \par \uc2  European stocks looked set to bounce, aided by the rebound in the United States and on the view the market had overreacted to a shock warning from Nokia. \par \par \uc2  "The late bounce in New York is a real relief to the market here," said Tsuyoshi Segawa, equity strategist at Shinko Securities in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks closed mixed on Tuesday as bargain-hunting set in after shares were jolted early in the session by a bleak growth forecast from Finland's Nokia Corp . \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average finished up 26.29 points or 0.24 percent at 10,948.38 after dropping more than one percent and the tech-laden NASDAQ Composite Index ended down 0.83 point or 0.04 percent at 2,169.95. \par \par \uc2  "There was a negative consensus out there with Nokia and other downgrades and so we got to levels that were compelling to investors," said Jim Herrick, managing director in charge of listed trading at Robert W. Baird & Co. "There was bargain hunting coming in." \par \par \uc2  In Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, the dollar gained ground against the yen, climbing to a three-week high of 122.39 yen from 121.62 in New York and the euro was supported above 0.8500 against the dollar amid worries of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  SAGS AT CLOSE \par \par \uc2  Selling of high-tech issues in late trading amid lingering concerns over the weakening economy pushed Tokyo shares into the negative territory. \par \par \uc2  "Signs the domestic economy is in trouble and worries the NASDAQ could dip further had investors quick to unload techs before the final bell," said Hidenori Karaki, equities general manager at Tokyo Mitsubishi Personal Securities. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the share market was higher on short covering following the sharp losses on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Shares in Hong Kong moved sideways as investors eyed the release in the United States of the May retail sales report later on Wednesday and inflation data on Thursday and Friday. \par \par \uc2  At 0625 GMT, the benchmark Hang Seng Index was down 0.06 percent at 13,518.87. \par \par \uc2  KOREA WEATHERS STRIKE \par \par \uc2  A nationwide labour walkout in Korea to demand pay rises and protest government-led restructuring entered its second day but failed to have a strong impact on the equity market. \par \par \uc2  Instead, Korean shares were nudged higher, led by bank and insurance stocks. \par \par \uc2  Boosting overall sentiment were signs of progress in a Hynix Semiconductor deal to attract up to $1.5 billion in foreign capital and smooth talks to sell bankrupt Daewoo Motor to General Motors . \par \par \uc2  The Korea Composite Stock Price Index (KOSPI) closed up 1.14 percent at 614.05. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan shares gave up early gains as profit taking on heavily weighted stocks and the weak Taiwan dollar dragged the market lower. \par \par \uc2  "With a falling Taiwan dollar, the main index could not hold onto earlier gains," said Eddy Chen, fund manager of Prudential Securities Investment Trust's Trust Fund. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar fell from 34.025 to a low of 34.280 against the U.S. due to worries over a slowing economy, the yen's weakness and a report that forecast the local unit would depreciate further. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark TAIEX closed down 56.53 points or 1.07 percent. \par \par \uc2  Singapore equities moved higher as a hostile cash offer in the financial sector stoked merger fervour in other sectors. \par \par \uc2  OCBC Bank , Singapore's third largest bank by market capitalisation, made an all-cash offer of S$4.8 billion ($2.65 billion) on Tuesday for Keppel Capital, the city states's smallest bank and a unit of diversified conglomerate Keppel Corp . \par \par \uc2  At 0615 GMT, the benchmark Straits Times Index was up 0.90 percent at 1,693.50. ((For the state of play of Asian stock markets please click on: )). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6d01hwt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
