\par\par \b \uc2  Jawboning The Euro Still Isn't Influencing Markets \b0\par\par\uc2 935 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2001\par 22:04\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  By John Hardy and Jennifer Downey \par \par \uc2  OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones--European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have turned up the rhetoric in support of the euro in the past seven days, but so far to little avail. \par \par \uc2  Europe's common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to languish just above $0.8500, roughly a cent higher than the low for 2001 reached Monday at $0.8412 and only three cents away from its all-time low at $0.8230 from October last year. \par \par \uc2  In fact, some observers are wondering whether the ECB has painted itself into a corner with all the jawboning. To keep the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from tumbling further, ECB officials have to keep reminding the market the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  supports a strong euro and is concerned with current levels. \par \par \uc2  But at some point, all the talk must be backed by action. That's what makes "verbal invention" effective-- the implicit threat of real invention to influence \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices, according to analysts. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must take action through buying euros and selling dollars to convince the market it means what its says. \par \par \uc2  In other words, the more ECB officials talk up the euro, the greater the likelihood they must resort to active \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  In recent days, ECB President Wim Duisenberg, Deutsche Bundesbank President and ECB council member Ernst Welteke, ECB Chief Economist Otmar Issing and several others have made pointed statements to attempt to prop up the euro. \par \par \uc2  While the volume of jawboning in support of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is nowhere near the levels seen in late 2000, ECB officials do appear to be again trying to "talk about it, to see if we can verbally support" the euro, as Chris Furness, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at 4Cast in London, commented. He sees this as a decisive shift up from nonchalant comments several weeks earlier. \par \par \uc2  The ECB's failure to convince markets contrast sharply with the Bank of Japan. A nod from BOJ or \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials usually has its desired effect on the yen. However, Japanese officials won their credibility in markets the hard way, spending an estimated $65 billion on a total of at least 15 \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1999 and 2000 to slow the yen's appreciation. \par \par \uc2  Whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the best possible counter to the yen's rise is open to debate, analysts said, but it certainly convinced markets that officials' words were backed by a very heavy stick. \par \par \uc2  The euro's tumble so far this year is eerily reminiscent of the period from mid-September to mid-November 2000, when the ECB was forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  four times-- once in concert with other major central banks and three times solo-- to arrest the euro's slide. \par \par \uc2  Although the point can never be proven, some observers wonder if all the strong talk in favor of the euro during the summer and autumn last year and its failure to prevent the euro's decline to an all-time low forced the ECB to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unilaterally in November. \par \par \uc2  In late May and early June this year, ECB President Wim Duisenberg and Belgian Finance Minister Didier Reynders sidestepped the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and denied concerns over the euro's external value. \par \par \uc2  The market was not impressed. "We thought that was a setup," said Robert Podorefsky, chief derivatives strategist at FleetBoston and traders took the comments as an invitation to sell the euro. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday, Welteke upped the ante, saying that risks to price stability from the euro's exchange rate need to be closely monitored. "I want to emphasize here again our strong interest in a strong euro," Welteke said, "the euro's exchange rate is a very important indicator within the second pillar of our monetary policy," he added. \par \par \uc2  Welteke's comments have so far helped support a two-day euro spike triggered partly by reported euro buying from the Austrian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which scared markets into thinking \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was nigh. \par \par \uc2  These jumpy markets reflect a stage in ECB jawboning "when members of the ECB do get a certain amount of success," Furness said. \par \par \uc2  But when markets get complacent and revert to selling euros, rhetoric enters the next stage, Furness said. "They start saying 'We will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Look, we're going to do it."' \par \par \uc2  "I think they'll get louder and will speak more often," said Furness, who expects ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the Group of Eight meeting in Genoa in late July. \par \par \uc2  Podorefsky noted that retail and corporate sentiment is shifted so far toward euro weakness that the euro may be able to benefit from short-covering and gain naturally. \par \par \uc2  Of course, that could also be a good time for the ECB to step in and add to any euro recovery. "When you've set such low expectations, that's when you get most bang for your buck," Podorefsky said. \par \par \uc2  In late New York trading, the euro was at $0.8532, lower than $0.8545 in London and little changed from $0.8528 late Tuesday in New York. The dollar was at Y122.10, slightly below London levels of Y122.20 but above Tuesday's Y121.64. Sterling was at $1.3879, above $1.3770 in London. \par \par \uc2  There is no doubt that many analysts are primed for the worst. Larry Greenberg, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Ried, Thunberg commented, "we believe that last October's historical $0.8230 low must be taken out before an enduring uptrend can occur, and fundamental support (for the euro) has eroded." \par \par \uc2  - By John Hardy; 201-938-2122; Dow Jones Newswires; john.hardy@dowjones.com. \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010710dx6d00rr6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
