\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Options market reveals  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  outlook. \b0\par\par\uc2  by Kyle Peterson \par \uc2 738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 2001\par 21:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, June 1 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options traders do not necessarily believe the hype, but talk of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has revived volatility in euro options, revealing a growing consensus that the euro soon could make a big move in one direction or another. \par \par \uc2  Since scoring a six-month low of 9.70 percent on May 18, implied volatility for front-end euro options has returned to near 12 percent quoted on Friday. Implied volatility is a statistical measure of how much the options market expects the price of the underlying asset to move during the life of the option. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts linked the increased volatility to the chance that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could wade into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and buy the beleaguered euro with the intention of increasing its value. \par \par \uc2  "Implied front-end vols are higher," said Ken Boyer, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at S&P's MMS. "It's obviously a big risk. I guess the key would be to be long front-end volatility." \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the one-month 25-delta risk reversal favored euro puts 0.6/0.2, up a touch from 0.5/0.1 quoted on Thursday. Risk reversals are the measure of the volatility bias of puts vs. calls. Meanwhile, the euro tumbled to a six-month low of $0.8431, a little more than 2 cents from its record low reached last Oct. 26. \par \par \uc2  Finance officials in Europe and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  have said in recent days that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not necessary or appropriate right now. But traders know there are no guarantees when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions and that the ECB is no stranger to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Sept. 22, the ECB joined other Group of Seven central banks in a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that saw the euro blast off from near $0.8600 to what was then an 18-day high of $0.9022 in just a few minutes. Analysts estimated that the central banks of Europe, Japan, the United States, England and Canada spent about $3 billion to force the euro higher that day. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair, however, failed to hold those gains and fell back to $0.8753 by the end of the U.S. trading session. The ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  three times more on its own in November, but those \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were not coordinated with other central banks from industrialized nations. Many analysts declared the series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be unsuccessful, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  resumed its lifelong downtrend. \par \par \uc2  OPTIONS STRATEGIES \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  options traders said some large banks are crafting options strategies with an eye toward \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In many cases, these options plays increase the likelihood of the euro falling. \par \par \uc2  Many banks have "one-touch" options starting at $0.8400 and every 50 points down to $0.8200, said Enrico Caruso, deputy chief dealer at MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He said $0.8250 is an especially popular one-touch strike. \par \par \uc2  One-touch options are neither puts nor calls, but directional plays. Holders of one-touch options pay a premium, hoping that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will reach a predetermined strike price. If the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reaches that price, the buyer can recoup the premium plus a profit. The euro one-touch options generally reflect the bearish leanings of the banks that hold them. \par \par \uc2  "All the big banks have those one-touches and they're all going to try to get those triggered," Caruso said. "That's going to put pressure on the downside and probably force the central banks' hand." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said such strategies indicate either that the ECB and other central banks will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  or that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains will not stick and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will reach their strike price without hindrance. \par \par \uc2  "They feel pretty certain that they can get some kind of a payout between here and there," said Alan Revercomb, options strategist at IDEAglobal. \par \par \uc2  Euro trade also is taking direction from basic knock-in options with triggers on the downside and options to the upside, Revercomb said. A knock-in option gives the holder a right to trade an amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a set price only if the barrier price is reached. If the pair fails to reach the trigger before the expiration, then the option expires worthless. \par \par \uc2  Revercomb said the strategy reflects a trader's anticipation of a "dip and a rally." But overall, "there's been some interest in downside strikes in general," he said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6102jtr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
