\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro slides on Japan remarks, U.S. data eyed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 2001\par 13:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 1 (Reuters) - The euro dropped below 100 yen for the first time in five months on Friday, as the yen extended broad gains which began in Asia after Japan's Finance Minister said recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves did not warrant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The comments by Masajuro Shiokawa also pressured the dollar against the yen, with the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest lurch higher against the euro triggering a move to new three-month lows on dollar/yen, which was down 0.5 percent in the run up to the U.S. non-farm payrolls data at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa's comments came a day after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Council member Klaus Leibscher and ECB head Wim Duisenberg appeared to rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the euro, sending the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to six-month lows against the dollar on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "The comments are partly a reason for the yen's strength as they suggest there is little concern about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from either European or Japanese officials," said Mitul Kotecha, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell below 100 yen for the first time since December 19 according to Reuters data. It had recovered to around 100.55 by 1146 GMT but was still down more than half a yen from the day's peaks. \par \par \uc2  But the euro edged up about a quarter percent against the dollar as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell faster than the euro against the yen. The dollar fell as far as 118.32 yen, its weakest since March 6 and down almost one percent from Friday's highs. \par \par \uc2  "There is a lot of selling pressure coming from euro/yen and I am not surprised as Japanese investors are concerned about the euro," said Neil Parker, market strategist at RBS Financial Markets in London. "This is weighing on dollar/yen." \par \par \uc2  U.S. DATA EYED \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters expect, on average, May U.S. non-farm payrolls will show a decline of 17,000, against a 223,000 fall in April. The jobless rate is expected to rise to 4.6 percent from 4.5 percent the month before. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said there was a risk the numbers could be weaker than expected after data on Thursday showed the number of Americans filing for continuous unemployment benefits surged to a 7-1/2 year high. \par \par \uc2  The National Association of Purchasing Management's May manufacturing survey, meanwhile, was due for release at 1400 GMT, with the headline index forecast at 43.5 against 43.2 in April. \par \par \uc2  The euro showed little reaction to Purchasing Managers' surveys released earlier from France, Italy and Germany, which showed manufacturing output falling in all three countries in May and French consumer confidence also fell in May. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. payrolls are the key piece of data for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets today," said Parker from RBS. \par \par \uc2  "But a weak number doesn't necessarily mean a weak dollar." \par \par \uc2  He added that weaker-than-expected payrolls data last month lifted the dollar after an initial fall as expectations rose that another U.S. rate cut would help boost growth. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was steady after Bank of France Governor Jean-Claude Trichet earlier said the euro-zone harmonised inflation (HICP) figures for May could show signs of short-term price pressures in the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  NOTE OF CAUTION \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that while comments by senior European and Japanese officials appeared to lower the chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such action should not be ruled out given the euro's heavy falls and concerns in Japan that a strong yen will hurt the already fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  "Shiokawa's comments have led to a feeling that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely soon," said Arjun Mittal, economist at American Express Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "But we would say that this does not preclude the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Traders added the ECB was also likely to get jittery if the euro fell further, given the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mandate for keeping inflation under control. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB's objective is price stability so the euro must be a concern despite recent comments suggesting that it isn't," said a London-based trader. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yen's apparent strength could also be seen as an adjustment of excessive weakness since April, which had largely stemmed from assumptions that the Japanese government would guide the yen down. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6102jdd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
