\par\par \b \uc2  Euro salved, bears singed in  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  scare. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 925 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2001\par 08:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 30 (Reuters) - The euro snatched some rare gains on Wednesday after bears were burnt by a brief, but painful, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  scare. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been on its knees in early trading with speculative sellers gunning for support at 102.40 yen and $0.8530, as well as 119.80 on dollar/yen. \par \par \uc2  But the tables were turned when a major German bank started buying euro/yen aggressively, sparking talk that it was acting on behalf of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sending the euro a full yen higher in a matter of minutes. \par \par \uc2  At roughly the same time a Japanese city bank barged in buying dollars for yen, lifting the greenback up ) yen to 120.60. With bids flying, the more high-strung in the market jumped to the conclusion that this was the Bank of Japan buying as part of a coordinated strike with the ECB. \par \par \uc2  However, those with cooler heads had other ideas and when the buying burst faded with no follow-up, the talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon withered. \par \par \uc2  "It wasn't sustained enough to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," argued a U.S. bank dealer. "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in particular likes to buy in wave after wave, not just a few bucks and that's it." \par \par \uc2  He suspected the euro/yen bid may have been just an options play since a large amount of positions with a strike at 103.00 are rolling off today. \par \par \uc2  "But it does show how nervous people are. The market's still keen to take out stops at 85 cents but we might have to play footsie for a while longer now." Which was why the euro was dithering at $0.8575 in late trade having been as low as $0.8536 and as high as $0.8592. \par \par \uc2  It was holding at 103.20 yen after its bounce from 102.40 but fresh Japanese offers were lurking as near as 103.40 and sell orders were stacked up at $0.8610 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar itself had settled at 120.30 yen after its scrape with 119.90, leaving it a shade firmer from New York. \par \par \uc2  The yen also won a late filip when IMF Managing Director Horst Koehler said that a weak yen was not the way for Japan to overcome its economic difficulties. \par \par \uc2  There is a body of opinion that argues Japan should deliberately weaken the yen to help promote exports. \par \par \uc2  STILL LEAKING MONEY \par \par \uc2  Some linked the euro's rise to the collapse of merger talks between French telecom equipment maker Alcatel and U.S. Lucent Technologies Inc and the idea that Alcatel would no longer need to buy dollars for the deal. \par \par \uc2  However, dealers noted that deal would have been an all-share offer so Alcatel never have had a need to buy dollars direct and in any case it would have taken months to get approval. \par \par \uc2  It was true that rumours of the deal had been viewed as a negative for the euro in that it highlighted how euro zone companies were still keener to invest in the United States than at home. \par \par \uc2  The collapse of the talks was thus a slight positive, but cold comfort given that data out from the ECB on Tuesday showed direct investment flows out of the euro zone leapt to 86 billion euros in the first quarter, double that of Q1 last year. \par \par \uc2  Furthermore, much of the flow seemed to be European firms investing money in U.S. subsidiaries brought in previous years. \par \par \uc2  Thus even the sharp falloff in cross border acquisitions seen this year was not producing the slowdown in euro outflows that many analysts had expected. \par \par \uc2  UNFLATTERING COMPARISONS \par \par \uc2  Neither was the euro helped by the run of economic data at the moment. Figures from France Tuesday showed businesses were planning to invest less and a survey of business confidence due later Wednesday was expected to reveal a further deterioration after hitting 19-month lows in April. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, data on U.S. consumer confidence out Tuesday showed a surprisingly strong bounce to 115.5 in May from 109.9. \par \par \uc2  Furthermore, some analysts have been revising up their forecasts for the crucial U.S. employment report on Friday due to the introduction of new seasonal adjustment factors. \par \par \uc2  One analyst also estimated the change would result in the combined payrolls drop reported for March and April being revised to just 132,000 from the original 267,000 fall. \par \par \uc2  Such an outcome would do much to soothe market worries about the risk of recession in the United States and so support the dollar. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0541 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab      Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2000 Cls\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   29 May.\par \tab \tab \tab      -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr\tab \tab   0.8578   0.8554   +0.28    -9.67   0.9496\par  Dlr/yen\tab \tab    120.31   120.14   +0.14    +5.12   114.45\par  Euro/yen\tab \tab   103.20   102.79   +0.40    -5.07   108.71\par  Dlr/swiss\tab \tab  1.7772   1.7818   -0.26   +10.99   1.6012\par  Stg/dlr\tab \tab    1.4240   1.4230   +0.07    -5.07   1.5000\par  Can/dlr\tab \tab    1.5364   1.5360   +0.03    +3.02   1.4914\par  Aus/dlr\tab \tab    0.5193   0.5169   +0.46    -7.17   0.5594\par  Euro/swiss\tab \tab 1.5251   1.5248   +0.02    +0.20   1