\par\par \b \uc2  Euro struggles as  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  hopes fade. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 581 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2001\par 05:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 30 (Reuters) - The euro struggled to cling to early morning gains on Wednesday after a bout of rallying met strong selling pressure amid worries over the euro-zone economy. \par \par \uc2  At midday, the euro was slightly up against the dollar at 85.75/80 cents, compared with 85.54 cents in late New York trade. Against the yen it stood at 103.22/40 yen, up from 102.79 yen in late New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar firmed to 120.44/49 yen from 120.14 yen in late New York trade. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had jumped to near 86 cents in early morning trade, driven by bids from at least two European operators. The dollar also vaulted to 120.55 yen on the back of the euro's rise. \par \par \uc2  The rapid movement fuelled speculation the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) could be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, but that turned out to be misplaced when the rises proved short-lived. \par \par \uc2  There was also talk that the euro's rise was related to the collapse of merger talks between French telecom equipment maker Alcatel and U.S. Lucent Technologies Inc \par \par \uc2  The French company, no longer needing dollars to buy Lucent, might be keen to offload the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Others were less certain, saying the news had already been out in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  Whatever the reason, the euro's prospects looked bleak after Hans-Werner Sinn, president of influential think tank Ifo, said a forecast of 2.1 percent growth in Germany was looking "shaky", dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In April, Ifo and five other institutions revised their joint growth projection to 2.1 percent from 2.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  The only hope for the euro for the moment is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB), or possibly joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with other central banks, they said. \par \par \uc2  ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  But there is a growing sense in the market that such action might not be imminent because European leaders seem less concerned about the euro's fall than they were last autumn when the ECB did step in to lift the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Apart from ECB President Wim Duisenberg and a few officials from European central banks, European policymakers have been conspicuously silent on the euro's woes, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Last autumn, various European policymakers expressed concern over the euro's decline but this time few of them have talked about it," said Toshio Sugita, manager at Nissho Iwai Corp. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the euro was around 84 to 85 cents last year. But maybe the environment is different now. The ECB seems unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon." \par \par \uc2  A senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at a European bank agreed. \par \par \uc2  "Last September, European leaders criticised the euro's fall. We will see a similar situation only if the euro falls further toward 80 cents, and that will be when the ECB actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Speculation is rife that the BOJ could also be interested in propping up the euro, given that a weak single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to hurt Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese authorities seem to want to protect the 100 yen level for the euro," said the European banker. \par \par \uc2  But the BOJ is unlikely to act on its own in light of lingering concerns among other Asian countries that an excessively weak yen will hurt their economies, he said. \par \par \uc2  A majority of dealers said the BOJ would interve only when the ECB decided to do so. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5u02t63\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
