\par\par \b \uc2  Speculative bets raise prospect of  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2001\par 16:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 30 (Reuters) - Signs that speculators are turning against the euro for the first time since last September's G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could give firepower to a new euro rescue mission, analysts say. The latest report from the Chicago Mercantile Exchange shows the speculative part of the market established its first net short euro position last week since mid-September and - at 5,453 contracts - its largest net short position since early April 2000. \par \par \uc2  The timing is significant, not least because the last time the speculative community went short of euros was just days before the Group of Seven industrial nations embarked on a euro-buying spree. \par \par \uc2  "Any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is clearly more effective if speculators are caught short," said Steven Saywell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  "The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would like to hurt as many speculative traders as possible and, if they could squeeze out short positions, they would get more for their money." \par \par \uc2  The position of speculative traders is seen as an accurate gauge of market sentiment as they are able to respond more quickly to change than commercial traders, who are constrained by specific client needs. \par \par \uc2  A bearish combination of slowing euro zone growth and rising inflation sent the euro crashing to six-month lows below $0.85 last week. This is within three cents of its all-time low and below levels which first prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  ALERT \par \par \uc2  The speed of the euro's recent fall has prompted warnings from both Europe and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a weapon at their disposal. \par \par \uc2  The ECB made three solo forays into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in November and is expected to be the front line of any further defensive action. Japan - keen to avoid further export-crimping yen gains when its economy is struggling - is also viewed as a willing helper. \par \par \uc2  A demonstration of how seriously traders view the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came earlier on Wednesday when aggressive buying of euro/yen by a German bank triggered speculation it was acting on behalf of the ECB, sending the euro a full yen higher in a matter of minutes. \par \par \uc2  At about the same time a Japanese bank was spotted buying dollars for yen, fuelling talk the Bank of Japan was acting in concert and lifting the dollar three-quarters of a yen. The talk proved groundless, but traders remain on guard for official action at any time. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that speculators are positioned against the euro for only the second time in a year gives the ECB a bit of leverage if it wants to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Abn Amro. \par \par \uc2  "We would certainly expect some action if the euro's record lows were threatened". \par \par \uc2  TIMING RIGHT? \par \par \uc2  But some analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can only be effective if the market has already turned in the desired direction. \par \par \uc2  They argue the ECB can ill afford to see its credibility further damaged by a botched attempt and may wait to ride a wave of more positive sentiment, such as it did in November when it was able to harness mounting concern over the U.S. economic downturn. \par \par \uc2  With the latest economic data suggesting the United States economy may rebound later in the year, while the euro zone has only just entered the slowdown tunnel, analysts say the prospects of newsflow turning in the euro's favour in the near future are slim. \par \par \uc2  "Although \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more effective if traders are caught short, the ECB will want to wait until it is running with the tide," said Mark Henry, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at GNI. \par \par \uc2  "From a sentiment perspective, data showing speculators building short positions could argue against the ECB stepping in just now." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5u02qs7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
