\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro wins reprieve from  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  wariness. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2001\par 09:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Updates price with fresh quotes, changes dateline and headline) \par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 28 (Reuters) - The euro rose almost a quarter percent against the yen on Monday and was steady against the dollar as wariness of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action discouraged bears from launching another assault on the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  These concerns were stoked earlier by Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda who said that, taking into account economic fundamentals, there was a need for the euro to recover against the dollar and the yen. \par \par \uc2  With the euro holding a cent above last week's six-month lows against the dollar and three yen above five-month lows against the yen, the market wants to see if European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg echoes similar concerns in a speech before the European parliament's economic committee. \par \par \uc2  "There are expectations the ECB and the BOJ could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which could help the euro," said Philip Meyer, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Societe Generale in Paris. \par \par \uc2  "But the markets will be calm today with the U.S. and UK closed." \par \par \uc2  The euro was hovering around $0.86 and 104.20 yen ahead of Duisenberg's speech later in the day. \par \par \uc2  ECB NOT ALONE \par \par \uc2  Since Friday, the euro has clawed back some of the three percent losses against the dollar and eight percent losses against yen it racked up last week, helped by comments from the ECB council member Ernst Welteke on Friday. \par \par \uc2  In a German newspaper Welteke was reported as saying that the euro's weakness has contributed to driving up inflation and that the exchange rate was an important factor for the ECB. \par \par \uc2  The euro's steep fall against the yen has also raised the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  either alone or in conjunction with the ECB to support the euro and restrain the yen and Kuroda's comments were a reminder the ECB was not alone, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Some traders suggested the central banks could be minded to take advantage of the lack of liquidity on a holiday to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  later on Monday, so getting more bang for their buck. \par \par \uc2  But others said any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely a long way off. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB is not like the Bank of Japan (BOJ)," said a trader at a Japanese bank in Tokyo. "It doesn't let people know when it plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so it will probably happen when everyone has forgotten about it." \par \par \uc2  The yen also fell against the dollar, trading around 121 yen per dollar, down about a quarter percent. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda also said he had not changed his mind that recent moves in dollar/yen were inappropriate. The dollar hit a three-month low around 118.80 yen last week having recoiled sharply from 124.00. \par \par \uc2  LOSS OF FAITH \par \par \uc2  The immediate focus was on the testimony by Duisenberg, but some traders were inclined to be cynical about the significance of his words. \par \par \uc2  "I can't see the euro going up just because the ECB President says something. Until \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually happens, it will be difficult to buy the euro," said the Japanese bank dealer in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Their attitude is due in part to the fact that many Japanese investors were caught out by the euro's steep fall last week. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people were caught under-hedged and the fear is that the euro will keep falling and cause some serious \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses," said a U.S. bank dealer in Tokyo, noting that Japanese investors were all too familiar with such behaviour. \par \par \uc2  "So a lot of these guys are praying for a bounce to sell into. That'll cap the upside and could make a slide self-fulfilling," he added. \par \par \uc2  Another cause of the euro's woes has been a run of surprisingly high inflation numbers in the euro zone coupled with disappointing economic growth data. \par \par \uc2  This triggered talk of stagflation, though many analysts consider this way overdone given that past episodes of stagflation have boasted inflation rates of 10 percent of more while the present rate was nearer three percent. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, in so much as this topped the ECB's self-imposed, and perhaps needlessly ambitious, inflation target of 2.0 percent it did limit the scope for further policy easing to support economic growth. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5s02yu3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
