\par\par \b \uc2  Euro relies on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fear to stay afloat. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 982 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2001\par 02:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 28 (Reuters) - The euro was listing lower on Monday with only market paranoia about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and holiday-thinned conditions keeping it afloat. \par \par \uc2  The floundering \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had got a modest boost on Friday when the market began to ponder the chance of action from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly as the euro was down at levels where it did \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  last November. \par \par \uc2  And on Monday morning the market got a reminder that the ECB is not alone in its concern over the euro. \par \par \uc2  Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda said that, taking into account economic fundamentals, there was a need for the euro to recover against the dollar and the yen. \par \par \uc2  He added he had not changed his mind that recent moves in dollar/yen were inappropriate. The dollar hit a three-month low around 118.80 yen last week having recoiled sharply from 124.00. That raised the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  either alone or in conjunction with the ECB to support the euro and restrain the yen. \par \par \uc2  Ever-suspicious traders also noted that with U.K. and U.S. markets on holiday, the central banks could be minded to take advantage of the lack of liquidity to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so getting more bang for their buck. \par \par \uc2  Even so, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dipped to $0.8585 in late morning trade after it bounced to $0.8608 in New York from six-month lows around $0.8495. \par \par \uc2  It remained fragile on the yen at 103.49 yen having suffered a gut-wrenching slide to five-month lows near 101.00 last week. M&A flows also kept it pressured on sterling at 60.40 pence after a fall from 61.00 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was resting at 120.55 yen having finished at 120.60 in New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  INFLATION TRAP \par \par \uc2  The immediate focus was on testimony by ECB President Wim Duisenberg before the European Parliament's economic committee later on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Traders will be keen to see if he shares the concerns of ECB board member Ernst Welteke about the weak euro's impact on inflation, a warning that first touched the market's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  nerve on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Despite the air of caution, dealers were still bearish on the euro overall in part because its indecently hasty retreat against the yen had caught many Japanese investors by surprise. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people were caught under-hedged and the fear is that the euro will keep falling and cause some serious \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses," said a U.S. bank dealer, noting that Japanese investors were all too familiar with such behaviour. \par \par \uc2  "So a lot of these guys are praying for a bounce to sell into. That'll cap the upside and could make a slide self-fulfilling," he added. \par \par \uc2  Another cause of the euro's woes has been a run of surprisingly high inflation numbers in the euro zone coupled with disappointing GDP data. \par \par \uc2  This in turn triggered talk of stagflation, though many analysts consider this way overdone given that past episodes of stagflation have boasted inflation rates of 10 percent of more while the present rate was nearer three percent. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, in so much as this topped the ECB's self-imposed, and perhaps needlessly ambitious, inflation target of 2.0 percent it did limit the scope for further policy easing to support economic growth. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, the Federal Reserve, which has no specific numerical target for inflation, has been able to chop rates five times even though U.S. inflation is also around 3.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  And the market is still banking on at least one more easing from the Fed, probably at its next meeting later this month. \par \par \uc2  That expectation may be refined by the splurge of U.S. data this week which culminates with the key employment report for May on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Any repeat of the shock 223,000 fall in payrolls reported in April would sorely test the market's confidence in a speedy economic recovery and could hurt the dollar. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0224 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2000 Cls \par \par \uc2  25 May. \par \par \uc2  ----------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2  Euro/dlr   0.8587   0.8608   -0.24    -9.57   0.9496 \par \par \uc2  Dlr/yen    120.58   120.60   -0.02    +5.36   114.45 \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen   103.52   103.81   -0.28    -4.77   108.71 \par \par \uc2  Dlr/swiss  1.7774   1.7736   +0.21   +11.00   1.6012 \par \par \uc2  Stg/dlr    1.4219   1.4220   -0.01    -5.21   1.5000 \par \par \uc2  Can/dlr    1.5461   1.5450   +0.07    +3.67   1.4914 \par \par \uc2  Aus/dlr    0.5197   0.5203   -0.12    -7.10   0.5594 \par \par \uc2  Euro/swiss 1.5267   1.5260   +0.05    +0.30   1.5221 \par \par \uc2  Euro/stg   0.6035   0.6047   -0.20    -4.58   0.6325. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0224 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2  Last    