\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro mixed, eyes Duisenberg testimony. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2001\par 14:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 28 (Reuters) - The euro slipped around a quarter percent against the dollar on Monday, hugging a tight range in holiday-thinned trade, as caution set in ahead of a key speech by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg. \par \par \uc2  But the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose a quarter percent against a broadly retreating yen after Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda said there was a need for the euro to recover against the dollar and the yen. His comment kept markets wary of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the euro. \par \par \uc2  With the euro less than a cent above last week's six-month lows against the dollar and three yen above five-month lows against the yen, markets want to see if Duisenberg echoes Kuroda's concerns in testimony to the European parliament's economic committee at 1300 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "Obviously Duisenberg will be the factor to watch," said Teis Knuthsen, chief strategist at SEB Merchant Banking in Copenhagen. \par \par \uc2  "Japan wants to stop the yen strengthening and this makes sense. The ECB wants a stronger euro but this does not make sense given the relative health of the (euro zone) economy." \par \par \uc2  British markets were shut on Monday for the Spring Bank Holiday, and U.S. markets were closed for the U.S. Memorial Day holiday. \par \par \uc2  At 1133 GMT, the euro was trading around $0.8590, stuck within a 1/3 cent trading range. It was holding at around 104 yen. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  TALK HEATS UP \par \par \uc2  The yen also fell against the dollar, trading around 121 yen per dollar, down more than a quarter percent. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda also said he had not changed his mind that recent moves in dollar/yen were inappropriate. The dollar hit a three-month low around 118.80 yen last week, having recoiled sharply from 124.00. \par \par \uc2  Since Friday, the euro has clawed back some of the three percent losses against the dollar and eight percent losses against the yen it racked up last week, helped by comments from ECB council member Ernst Welteke on Friday. \par \par \uc2  In a German newspaper Welteke was reported as saying that the euro's weakness had contributed to inflation and that the exchange rate was an important factor for the ECB. \par \par \uc2  The euro's steep fall against the yen has also raised the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , either alone or in conjunction with the ECB, to support the euro and restrain the yen, and Kuroda's comments were a reminder the ECB was not alone, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers suggested the central banks could take advantage of the lack of liquidity on a holiday to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  later on Monday, so getting more bang for their buck. \par \par \uc2  But others said this was unlikely. \par \par \uc2  "There is talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but I think it is unlikely today and central banks are more likely to do it when the euro is going up," said a trader at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  "I don't expect Duisenberg to give any surprises away on euro/dollar thinking." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Duisenberg's comments would also be scrutinised for clues on the ECB's thinking on interest rates, given its unexpected rate cut earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  Recent data has also shown that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's room for manoeuvre is limited given that inflation remains high. The bank left rates on holds at its latest meeting last Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The main cause of the euro's recent woes has been a run of surprisingly high inflation numbers in the euro zone coupled with disappointing economic growth data. \par \par \uc2  This triggered talk of stagflation, though many analysts consider this overdone given that past episodes of stagflation have boasted inflation rates of 10 percent of more while the present rate was nearer three percent. \par \par \uc2  Data earlier on Monday showed German import price inflation rose to a higher-than-expected 5.1 percent in April year-on-year from 4.2 percent in March, indicating that price pressures in Europe's largest economy are not abating. \par \par \uc2  "The market will be looking for Duisenberg to explain the reasons behind the last rate cut," said SEB's Knuthsen. \par \par \uc2  "There is very little he can do to help the euro, which is suffering from a number of short-term factors." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5s02y94\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
