\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro stirred by persistent  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  talk. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2001\par 17:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 28 (Reuters) - The euro gained about a quarter percent against the yen on Monday but was steady against the dollar as a senior Japanese official's remarks kept alive wariness of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action in support of the euro. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  however showed little reaction to European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg's remarks to a European Parliamentary committee that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a tool in the ECB's arsenal but would not comment ahead of such action. \par \par \uc2  The yen was the biggest loser, falling a third of a percent against the dollar, in the wake of comments from Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda who said there was a need for the euro to recover against the dollar and yen. \par \par \uc2  "I am not too surprised by Kuroda's comments and the prime suspect behind any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  "Duisenberg's comments are much of a non-event and this is why the euro hasn't done much." \par \par \uc2  In prepared remarks, Duisenberg justified this month's surprise rate cut, reiterating the ECB's governing council now no longer saw a future inflation risk from growth in money supply, while slower than expected economic growth should also "dampen upward pressure on consumer prices". \par \par \uc2  The main cause of the euro's recent woes has been a run of surprisingly high inflation numbers in the euro zone, coupled with disappointing economic growth data, which have been seen as limiting the scope for further monetary easing. \par \par \uc2  British markets were shut on Monday for the Spring Bank Holiday, and U.S. markets were closed for the U.S. Memorial Day holiday. \par \par \uc2  At 1513 GMT, the euro was trading around $0.8595, stuck within a 1/3 cent trading range. It was holding at around 104 yen, more than two yen above last week's five-month lows. \par \par \uc2  The yen was trading around 121 yen, down more than a quarter percent on the day. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  TALK CONTINUES \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said he had not changed his mind that recent moves in dollar/yen were inappropriate. The dollar hit a three-month low around 118.80 yen last week, having recoiled sharply from 124.00. \par \par \uc2  Since Friday, the euro has clawed back some of the three percent losses against the dollar and eight percent losses against the yen it racked up last week, helped by comments from ECB council member Ernst Welteke on Friday who said the euro's weakness had contributed to inflation and that the exchange rate was an important factor for the ECB. \par \par \uc2  The euro's steep fall against the yen has also raised the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , either alone or in conjunction with the ECB, to support the euro and restrain the yen, and Kuroda's comments were a reminder the ECB was not alone, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was most likely to come from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  given concerns that a strong yen could hurt an already weak economy. \par \par \uc2  "Japan wants to stop the yen strengthening and this makes sense. The ECB wants a stronger euro but this does not make sense given the relative health of the (euro zone economy)," saod Teis Knuthsen, chief strategist at SEB Merchant Banking in Copenhagen. \par \par \uc2  Recent data has also shown that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's room for manoeuvre is limited given that inflation remains high despite signs of slowing growth. The bank left rates on hold at its latest meeting last Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  This triggered talk of stagflation, though many analysts consider this overdone given that past episodes of stagflation have seen inflation rates of 10 percent of more while the present rate was nearer three percent. \par \par \uc2  Data earlier on Monday showed German import price inflation rose to a higher-than-expected 5.1 percent in April year-on-year from 4.2 percent in March, indicating that price pressures in Europe's largest economy are not abating. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5s02q5d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
