\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro bounces off lows as frenetic yen rally fades. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 452 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2001\par 12:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 24 (Reuters) - The euro clawed back more than one percent from six-month lows against the dollar and five-month lows against the yen on Thursday, as dealers grew wary of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to stem its rapid fall. \par \par \uc2  Fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained ground in holiday-thinned markets after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda said the euro's recent sharp fall was inappropriate and action would be taken if unnatural \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves continued. \par \par \uc2  The euro has lost as much as three percent against the dollar and eight percent against the yen this week as concerns have risen over the euro zone's growth outlook. Recent data have shown inflation pressure in the region gathering pace despite slowing growth. \par \par \uc2  "The market is likely to be wary about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially after Kuroda's comments," said Neil MacKinnon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB currently faces the worst of both worlds, with deteriorating activity and rising inflation, so the euro's downtrend is still in place." \par \par \uc2  As \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns gripped the market, the euro recovered 1-1/2 yen from five-month lows below 101.25 and almost a cent from earlier six-month lows around $0.85. Dealers said a market holiday in much of Europe meant trading volumes were thin. \par \par \uc2  YEN RALLY LOSES STEAM \par \par \uc2  Kuroda's comments contrasted with earlier remarks from Japan's Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, who was quoted as saying he did not see exchange rates as a special problem. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa's apparently relaxed attitude to the yen's recent gains also lifted the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 11-week highs beyond 119 to the dollar in Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  "Kuroda is trying to make it clear that Japan wants a stable yen," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in London. \par \par \uc2  "He is clearing up confusion sown by Shiokawa's earlier comments." \par \par \uc2  In a frenetic overnight session, the yen became the beneficiary as U.S. hedge funds, as well as Japanese investors, bought yen against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Hedge funds had borrowed the yen earlier to take advantage of low Japanese interest rates and had invested that money in the euro, but were forced to close such positions in the face of recent falls in the euro, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "We have had stop-loss orders out of Japan which are weakening dollar/yen," said Teis Knuthsen, chief strategist at SEB Merchant Banking in Copenhagen. \par \par \uc2  But the yen's recent strength does not reflect any improvement in sentiment towards the Japanese economy, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "There is no news out of Japan which would justify a stronger yen," added Knuthsen. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5o037kv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
