\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar, euro jump against yen after Kuroda remarks. \b0\par\par\uc2 207 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2001\par 09:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 24 (Reuters) - The dollar and the euro racked up speedy gains of more than a quarter yen after comments by a senior Japanese Finance Ministry official stoked wariness of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Haruhiko Kuroda said action would be taken if unnatural \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves continued, noting that the recent sharp fall in the euro was inappropriate and that the dollar's exchange rate against the yen was also showing inappropriate moves and warranted monitoring. \par \par \uc2  His comments come in a week in which the euro has fallen to this year's lows against the dollar, the yen, and sterling, with the magnitude of its losses against the yen also dragging the dollar down to 11-week lows against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Kuroda is trying to make it clear that Japan wants a stable yen," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to peaks near 120 yen and the euro leapt to highs near 102.60 in the wake of Kuroda's comments. That compares with the dollar's earlier 11-week lows around 118.90 yen and the euro's five-month lows near 101.25 yen. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5o034bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
