\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 1-Asia shares bruised by Nasdaq beating, yen surges. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Valerie Lee \par \uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2001\par 09:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 24 (Reuters) - Stocks in Asia took a slight bruising on Thursday as Nasdaq's Wednesday fall revived investor selling in technology shares, while the yen retreated on talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We cannot help but get a head wind from abroad," said Kunihiro Hatae, general manager in Tokai Tokyo Securities' equities trading division. \par \par \uc2  "People have been keen to lock in profits from gains in high-tech stocks here in recent weeks," he added. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei share average gave up 1.22 percent or 171.91 points to finish at 13,895.79. \par \par \uc2  European shares also looked set to pull back following lower closes in the U.S. markets and with business light as many countries took time off for an Ascension Day holiday. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the Nasdaq Composite Index sank 3.04 percent to 2,243.50 as traders questioned the sharp rises on the tech-heavy index over the past six sessions amid grim outlooks from some U.S. software firms and fresh data showing a plunge in computer chip orders. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones average fell 1.35 percent to 11,105.51. \par \par \uc2  A surprise surge in the yen rippled through Japanese markets, denting already vulnerable stock prices and giving investors another reason to opt for the safety of government debt. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  raced to near three-month highs on the dollar and hit a five-month peak against the flailing euro, before giving up ground as inevitable rumours of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  cooled the market's fever. \par \par \uc2  The yen was trading at 119.87/90 against the dollar by 0612 GMT and at 102.61/2.71 against the euro. \par \par \uc2  TOKYO SLUMPS \par \par \uc2  The Tokyo share decline was the third straight. The stronger yen and the Nasdaq slide slapped back shares of bellwether techs and big exporters. \par \par \uc2  "Domestic institutions were looking for reasons to unwind a good slice of their long positions in the tech sector. The yen and the Nasdaq gave them two very good ones," said Masayoshi Okamoto, a trader at Jujiya Securities. \par \par \uc2  Electronics giant Sony dipped 2.45 percent to 9,950 yen, while Honda Motor Co Ltd ended down 3.44 percent at 5,060. \par \par \uc2  Semiconductor equipment makers found themselves under a negative spotlight from a bearish report on the sector by U.S.-based trade group Semiconductor Equipment and Materials International (SEMI). \par \par \uc2  TECH LOSSES HIT HK \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong shares slid by about one percent by midday as investors cashed in on tech shares. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Hang Seng index of 33 blue chips was down 1.03 percent at 13,696.84 points. \par \par \uc2  "Tech stocks took the cue from Nasdaq and fell but the market should stay fairly thin in the short term with the U.S employment figures out later tonight, and on Friday Hong Kong's revised GDP figures will be closely watched," said Miles Remington, head of Asian sales at SG Securities. \par \par \uc2  Shares in Taiwan weathered the blow of Nasdaq's overnight fall and closed slightly higher on Thursday on continued net buying by foreign funds and hopes for state fund support. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark TAIEX index stayed in a narrow 74-point range all session ending up 0.32 percent at 5,226.79. \par \par \uc2  SEOUL A SHADE DOWN \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, South Korea's bellwether stock index ended a shade lower driven by chip makers and Pohang Iron and Steel Co (POSCO) . \par \par \uc2  Trade held in a tight range, with strength in Hyundai Securities helping the market recoup early losses at the last minute on expectations for takeover talks between a U.S. insurance group and the government. \par \par \uc2  The Korea Composite Stock Price Index (KOSPI) shed 0.05 percent to close at 622.28, down 0.32 point. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5o03488\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
