\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro claws back ground on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  wariness. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 487 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2001\par 14:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 24 (Reuters) - The euro rebounded more than one percent from this year's lows against the dollar and the yen on Thursday as dealers grew wary of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to stem its rapid decline. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  jitters grew in holiday-thinned European markets after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda said the euro's recent sharp fall was inappropriate and that action would be taken if unnatural \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves continued. \par \par \uc2  His comments come after the euro has lost as much as three percent against the dollar and eight percent against the yen this week on signs that euro zone growth is slowing at a time when inflation pressures are limiting scope for interest rate cuts. \par \par \uc2  "The euro has had a sharp move lower and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become a distinct possibility," said Francesca Fornasari, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  "The last thing Japan needs is a strong yen at a time when it is trying to implement reform, while for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  a weak euro only exacerbates the inflation problem." \par \par \uc2  Gloom over the euro zone's economic outlook also lifted slightly on Thursday after data showed Italian consumer confidence rose to its highest in 11 years in May, boosted by hopes of political stability despite weakening economic growth across Europe. \par \par \uc2  In thin markets, due to a holiday in much of Europe, the euro recovered 1-1/2 yen from five-month lows below 101.25 yen and as much as a cent from earlier six-month lows around $0.85. \par \par \uc2  YEN RALLY LOSES STEAM \par \par \uc2  Kuroda's comments contrasted with earlier remarks from Japan's Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, who was quoted as saying he did not see exchange rates as a special problem. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa's apparently relaxed attitude to the yen's recent gains also knocked the dollar to 11-week lows below 119 yen in Asia. But the dollar recovered to near 120 yen by the European mid-session as traders reassessed Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance. \par \par \uc2  "Kuroda is trying to make it clear that Japan wants a stable yen," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in London. \par \par \uc2  "He is clearing up confusion sown by Shiokawa's earlier comments." \par \par \uc2  In a frenetic overnight session, the yen became the beneficiary as U.S. hedge funds, as well as Japanese investors, bought yen against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Hedge funds had borrowed the yen earlier to take advantage of low Japanese interest rates and had invested that money in the euro, but were forced to close such positions in the face of recent falls in the euro, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yen's recent strength did not reflect any improvement in sentiment towards the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  "There is no news out of Japan which would justify a stronger yen," said Teis Knuthsen, chief strategist at SEB Merchant Banking in Copenhagen. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5o033zr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
