\par\par \b \uc2  China Appears To Be Building Case For Yuan Devaluation \b0\par\par\uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2001\par 06:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  BEIJING -(Dow Jones)- China appears to be building a case for a yuan devaluation with a foreign trade ministry outlook released Friday warning of the likelihood of a shrinking trade surplus this year. \par \par \uc2  The release of the Ministry of Foreign Trade and Economic Cooperation, or Moftec, quarterly outlook comes only days after a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  report warned of the threat to China's competitiveness posed by a weakening yen. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say that unlike 1999, when the Clinton administration urged China to stand firm in maintaining the stability of the yuan, strained relations with Washington mean Beijing is less likely to heed such a request. Analysts also think the Bush administration isn't likely to repeat the joint U.S.-Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that previously rescued the yen. \par \par \uc2  As a fall in demand linked to the global economic slowdown is eroding China's trade position, its export competitiveness is being further undermined by a 10% appreciation of the yuan against Japan's yen and the South Korean won in recent months. \par \par \uc2  The Moftec outlook forecasts imports will grow faster than exports this year, leading to a narrower trade surplus. Trade figures released this year showed China's trade surplus has fallen to $5.4 billion in the first four months of this year from $7.3 billion in the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  "China needs to take measures to improve the competitiveness of its exports," the trade ministry's outlook says, without specifically mentioning a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation. \par \par \uc2  The report says one of the factors weighing on trade performance is the weakness of Japan's yen and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is eroding the price competitiveness of China's exports. The global economic slowdown and reemergence of trade protectionism also threatens to reduce China's exports this year, it says. \par \par \uc2  In a report issued Wednesday, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also warned that the continued weakening of the yen is having a negative influence on trade. \par \par \uc2  "The continued devaluation of yen will impose pressure on the stability of yuan," the People's Bank of China quarterly monetary policy report said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said demand for China's exports will fall due to the sluggish U.S. and Japanese economy, and that fall will also threaten the stability of the exchange rate. However, the report said an upward trend in foreign investment should help cushion some of those factors. \par \par \uc2  Though it didn't mention the possibility of a devaluation either, the PBOC report said, "China should continue a policy of expanding domestic demand, implement a proactive fiscal policy, maintain a stable and healthy monetary policy, overhaul and standardize the order of the market economy and expand the domestic market." \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only convertible on the trade account and is kept within a tight range relative to the dollar. Therefore, the yuan tends to appreciate against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in line with any strengthening in the greenback. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually trades in a narrow band around 8.27 yuan to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't overtly threaten a devaluation, analysts believe China is saber-rattling to prod Japan and the U.S. into action to prevent any further slip in the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's statement should be viewed as a veiled warning to U.S. and Japan to maintain the stability of the yen," DBS Bank analyst Philip Wee said in a research note. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But a Bank of America research note warned that the Bush\par administration is less likely to join with the Bank of Japan to prop up\par the yen. In 1998, the U.S. and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to push\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  back above 140 yen to the dollar. In 1995, the two\par countries, with the help of some European countries, also jointly\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to push the value of the then strong yen down against the\par dollar.\par    -By Owen Brown, Dow Jones Newswires; 8610 6588-5848;\par owen.brown@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx5i025hw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
