\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro backtracks, focus turns to U.S. stocks. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 810 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2001\par 14:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 17 (Reuters) - The euro fell by 1/4 percent against the dollar on Thursday, more than reversing earlier gains to one-week highs, as the market focused on the possibility of another sizzling performance by U.S. stocks. \par \par \uc2  Earlier euro buying, thought to be fund or merger-related, fizzled out, leaving traders to contemplate the positive impact of this week's half-point rate cut on U.S. assets, and therefore on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday we had an equity rally in the U.S. and that is at least why the dollar is not weakening any more against the euro today," said Sonja Hellemann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro built on gains made in the previous session to rise as far as $0.8880 and 109.30 yen early in Europe, before retreating to $0.8820 and 108.75 by 1145 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Talk a European \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was selling dollars for Swiss francs took the dollar briefly to a two-week low of 1.7266 francs. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also retreated from recent highs against the yen after a Japanese press report got traders talking anew about the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen weakness. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading around 123.20 yen, a little above the day's lows and nearly one yen below May highs set on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  EURO SPECULATION \par \par \uc2  The euro extended gains made on Wednesday and in Asia on Thursday on a large order to buy euros for dollars by a German bank, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Speculation about the reasons for Thursday's early gains varied from euro buying related to merger and acquisition activity, to fund management purchases and to commercial buying by European central banks. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the temporary nature of such flows meant the euro's rise might stall in the face of the Federal Reserve's aggressive monetary easing to boost the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial average jumped 3.15 percent to an eight-month high on Wednesday, in the wake of the Fed's fifth half-point rate cut on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The hi-tech Nasdaq index soared by 3.88 percent, and Nasdaq futures indicated a strong open on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the day's U.S. economic data would come under the spotlight as the market decides whether the Fed is likely to make another aggressive rate cut. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lowered rates five times this year, taking the federal funds rate to a seven-year low of 4.00 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Philadelphia Fed survey for May is due at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  INFLATION, WHAT INFLATION? \par \par \uc2  European policy-makers, meanwhile, attempted to play down data released this week showing euro-zone inflation at a five-month high close to three percent in April, well above the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's self-imposed two percent threshold. \par \par \uc2  Belgian finance minister and head of the euro group of finance ministers Didier Reynders said he was confident euro zone inflation would come in at a reasonable level this year. \par \par \uc2  ECB board member Klaus Liebscher said he expected the pick-up in inflation to be short-term, while Spanish Economy Minister Rodrigo Rato said European inflation pressure was "not historically high", given a rise in oil prices. \par \par \uc2  The White House chief economic adviser Glenn Hubbard also played down U.S. inflation levels saying they posed no problem this year or next despite the Fed's recent interest rate cuts. \par \par \uc2  There was no immediate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact from news that Russia and the European Union had agreed to study ways to boost the use of the euro in bi-lateral trade and economic ties. \par \par \uc2  YEN STRONG OR WEAK? \par \par \uc2  The yen was helped by an article in Japanese business daily Nihon Keizai saying that former Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa had discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a meeting of Asian finance ministers in April. \par \par \uc2  Analaysts said this had shifted the market focus back to whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's recent weakness. \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka said late on Wednesday that the yen was not far from a convenient level. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with Reuters, Takenaka also said the yen would be influenced by the painful restructuring Japan will have to undergo, which traders took as tacit acceptance of a softer \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yen was also helped on Thursday by a 1.58 percent rise in the benchmark Nikkei average, and data showed foreigners were net buyers of Japanese stocks last week for the eighth straight week. \par \par \uc2  But analysts added that, as with euro/dollar, moves in dollar/yen were likely to remain within recent broad ranges. \par \par \uc2  "It does not look like there is any enthusiasm for any of the G3 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Steven Englander, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5h03m3g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
