\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro gains steal spotlight as Fed effect wanes. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2001\par 14:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 16 (Reuters) - The euro rose as much as one percent against the yen and the dollar on Wednesday as corporate demand and talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  commercial orders overshadowed the previous day's U.S. interest rate cut. \par \par \uc2  However, the temporary nature of the commercial business transacted meant the euro had pared its gains by the European midsession as focus shifted to U.S. inflation data, due at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the inflation numbers would be eagerly watched for clues as to the Federal Reserve's scope for future interest rate cuts. \par \par \uc2  "The market is starting to give the Fed less benefit of the doubt over its handling of the U.S. economy," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns. \par \par \uc2  "A muted reaction from U.S. asset markets to the Fed rate cut has given little support to the dollar, while the euro has taken advantage of solid corporate demand." \par \par \uc2  Reports that France Telecom and Deutsche Telekom may be about to sell their joint stake in U.S. carrier Sprint - worth an estimated $3.7 billion - and talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  commercial orders to buy euros provided fuel for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's early rally. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $0.8840 at 1140 GMT, off its best levels but up over a cent from one-month lows plumbed earlier this week. The euro showed little reaction to data showing euro zone inflation rose sharply in April to levels not seen since November. \par \par \uc2  The fact that investors could now receive a greater premium for holding euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets over U.S. ones was also seen as a potential euro positive. The Fed's latest cut means euro zone interest rates are now half a percentage point above those in the United States. \par \par \uc2  YEN MALAISE \par \par \uc2  The yen was the biggest loser - falling to two-week lows against both the euro and the dollar - after Japan's Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa admitted to being increasingly worried about Japan's economic outlook and appeared relaxed about the recent fall in the yen. \par \par \uc2  As if to drive home the point, fresh figures showed Japanese bankruptcy debt was up nearly 11 percent in April from a year earlier while the Nikkei 225 stock average slid two percent to a three-week low. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa added that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be left to the market and present \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels were appropriate, comments traders took to mean the ministry was not unhappy with the recent slip in the yen and there was little risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent it. \par \par \uc2  "There is concern that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new prime minister has little room for policy manoeuvre ahead of elections in July and that a weaker yen may be the only real tool at his disposal," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  The euro shot over one percent to highs above 109.70 yen but was trading around half-a-yen short of its best levels by 1140 GMT. The dollar was trading at 123.60 yen up a quarter-percent on the day but off best levels above 124 yen set in Asia. \par \par \uc2  MUTED REACTION TO FED \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar had been discouraged by the muted response of U.S. assets to the Fed's 50 basis point cut, with equities flat and longer-dated Treasuries falling sharply. \par \par \uc2  Dollar bulls had also been hoping the Fed would be more upbeat on the economy in its statement, so supporting lingering expectations of a "V"-shaped recovery. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar has not done extremely well following the rate cut because the Fed's statement was not as optimistic about the economic outlook as the market had hoped", said Audrey Childe-Freeman, European economist at CIBC World Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  "The move was predictable but the market is a little bit disappointed with the statement." \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, the Fed has cut a blistering 250 basis points this year to take rates to seven-year lows and left the door open to yet further easings if needed. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has only shaved rates by 25 basis points and then found its credibility was badly damaged in the eyes of the market by the way in which it explained its move. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Bank of Japan has no rates left to cut and even its attempts at quantitative easing are meeting with scant success in a market which simply has no use for the extra cash. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx5g000vz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
