\par\par \b \uc2  Options traders braced for further yen declines. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 2001\par 16:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 27 (Reuters) - Options traders see a rocky ride for the yen amid growing concern Japan's new government may be more willing to tolerate a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen has staged its biggest decline against the dollar in over a month this week, as traders remained cautious over the appointment of veteran as Masajuro Shiokawa finance minister and indications of a more laissez-faire approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  Expectation of further yen losses were reflected in the options market where traders are now prepared to pay a premium for the right to sell yen against dollars for the next year and beyond. \par \par \uc2  "The options market has now priced in higher volatility and more downside for the yen," said Jesper Dannesboe, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein. \par \par \uc2  "The new government seems more willing to tolerate yen weakness, and the market is braced for a fairly sharp move lower in the near term." \par \par \uc2  Dannesboe said that the skew for risk reversals, or directional preference, suggested the yen would see a fairly sharp decline against the dollar over the next few weeks before steadying in a lower range, probably between 125 and 130 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Yen puts - which traded flat to yen calls earlier this week - were commanding at 0.5 percentage point premium on a one-week time horizon on Friday. One-month yen puts commanded a premium of 0.35 percentage points and one-year puts a 0.3 percentage points. \par \par \uc2  RINGING THE CHANGES \par \par \uc2  Enthusiasm as reform-minded Junichiro Koizumi took the hotseat of Japan's ruling party proved short-lived this week as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders focused both on the new prime minister's ability to deliver and on the short-term economic pain even if he did. \par \par \uc2  Japan's new finance minister accelerated the yen's losses on Thursday when he said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should be left to the market and not meddled with by governments - a distinct change in tone from the recent thinly-veiled \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threats of the previous government. \par \par \uc2  Reports \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Masaru Hayami, a strong advocate of a strong yen, intended to resign gave the yen a further knock on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The fallout from the government's reform programme and comments from Japanese officials all point in the direction of yen weakness," said Hans Redeker, strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "Yen risk reversals and the low level of yen short positions suggest the yen will weaken further." \par \par \uc2  Implied volatility - market expectations of future spot gyrations - on dollar/yen options has risen nearly a percentage point in the last two days. \par \par \uc2  GNI technical analyst Tom Nagle said recent price action meant the dollar was well placed to advance against the yen, but expected resistance at 127 to cap gains. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4r01a4d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
