\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar dips after Miyazawa comment on Asia impact. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2001\par 09:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 10 (Reuters) - Japan's repeated concerns over the yen's rapid depreciation depressed the dollar's rally in Tokyo on Tuesday morning, spurring hedge sales and corrective sales to pull the U.S. unit down well over a half yen from a morning high. \par \par \uc2  The dollar started losing ground in mid-morning after Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said he understood that Asian countries were concerned over the impact that the yen's weakness could have on the region's economy, though he also said he had no plans to control the yen's movements. \par \par \uc2  Separately, Miyazawa said he had no plans of guiding the yen lower in order to boost exports and help the Japanese economy recover from its current downturn. \par \par \uc2  Although many dealers were sceptical that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at present levels, they were reluctant to sell the yen strongly when Japanese officials were repeatedly expressing their concerns over the yen's rapid fall, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Overseas operators were also reluctant to chase the dollar on rallies ahead of Easter holidays this weekend. \par \par \uc2  "The basic fundamentals of the dollar are bullish but the market was becoming nervous about chasing it on a rally," said Masayuki Yamamoto, assistant vice president at the Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "Overseas players, who were turning reluctant to expand their positions ahead of Easter, unloaded long dollar positions," Yamamoto said. "The recent series of comments from the Japanese side has clearly pressured the dollar." \par \par \uc2  At 0300 GMT, the dollar was at 124.66/76 yen after climbing as high as 125.40 yen in early trade. It was at 125.01/09 yen in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  INVESTORS STILL NERVOUS \par \par \uc2  Dollar sentiment was also undermined due to an absence of active buying from Japanese institutional investors since the start of the new fiscal year on April 1, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Investors were originally expected to buy foreign securities actively to raise returns at a time when domestic interest rates are stuck at record low levels. But there have been no aggressive purchases since the beginning of April, they said. \par \par \uc2  Some institutions, such as trust banks, were detected to have loaded up on dollars heavily around the middle of March, but they were now being forced to sell to hedge against their positions because the dollar's gains have been limited in the past week or so. \par \par \uc2  "With a perception that the dollar would firm sharply towards 130 to 140 yen diminishing, some investors are intensifying their efforts to sell dollars for hedge purposes," a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at a city bank said. "Exporters were also selling more eagerly." \par \par \uc2  ECB EYED \par \par \uc2  Investors such as life insurers were soon expected to decide soon whether to start buying euros after confirming the outcome of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy-setting meeting on Wednesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "It is still uncertain whether investors will buy in large volume," a Japanese commercial bank dealer said. "If the euro's outlook becomes brighter after the ECB, then domestic investors will start pouring fresh funds into euros." \par \par \uc2  The euro was little changed against the yen and dollar as the market awaited the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy-setting meeting on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Most analysts expect at least a 25 basis point reduction in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 4.75 percent benchmark rate. \par \par \uc2  A rate cut is often negative for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but an actual credit easing by the ECB was expected to draw euro buying on the expectation that it would help boost economy in the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  The euro faced resistance above $0.90 in Tokyo, standing at $0.8960/67 against the previous late U.S. level of $0.8967/71. Against the yen, it was at 111.77/90 yen against 112.12/30 yen. \par \par \uc2  Sterling retained its overseas firmness in Tokyo, although follow-through buying was limited after its rise to near four-week highs against the dollar on Monday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was little changed at $1.4446/56 against $1.4448/56. It had traded as high as $1.4500 on Monday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4a02amv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
