\par\par \b \uc2  US Lindsey: Reiterates Bush Commitment To Strong Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 747 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2001\par 23:12\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON -(Dow Jones)- Lawrence Lindsey, President George W. Bush's chief economic adviser, has issued a detailed and determined defense of the so-called strong dollar policy in an article carried by the latest edition of The International Economy magazine. \par \par \uc2  In the article, Lindsey argues the "benefits of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for our country far outweigh the costs" and that the actions of the Bush administration since taking office in January reflect the new president's support for this key policy. \par \par \uc2  He also rejected the use of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an effective policy tool, citing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's experience at seeking to influence the yen and its indirect role behind the Asian financial crisis of 1997/98. \par \par \uc2  "Unilateral efforts to manipulate exchange rates have, in the past, contributed to misalignments in the dollar relative to other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly the yen," Lindsey observed. \par \par \uc2  "For example, the mispricing of exchange rates played a key role in skewing investment to third countries that were linked to the dollar, but which traded actively with Japan," he said. \par \par \uc2  "When these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  became more appropriately valued, these investments proved unprofitable. This played a part in the ensuing Asian financial crisis," Lindsey said. \par \par \uc2  Lindsey argued that America's interest in a strong dollar "is not academic," citing at least three tangible benefits to the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  The first is that the U.S. has become the "world's leading trading nation," thanks to its ability to trade primarily in its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Secondly, the $400 billion of Federal Reserve notes held outside the U.S. generate seignorage, which effectively represents $20 billion in saved interest payments a year, Lindsey said. \par \par \uc2  Perhaps the most evident benefit in recent years, he said, is that the strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made the U.S. an attractive place to invest. \par \par \uc2  "During the 1990s, the willingness of investors around the world to participate in our capital markets led to the greatest capital-spending boom in history," Lindsey said. \par \par \uc2  "That boom allowed America to make enormous technological advances in telecommunications infrastructure and computer technology. The surge in U.S. productivity during the late 1990s was no doubt attributable to that investment," he said. \par \par \uc2  The strong dollar policy was instituted in 1995 by former Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, and his senior advisers, particularly then-Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers. \par \par \uc2  Accordingly, there was speculation immediately following confirmation of Bush's election victory in December that the Republican administration might seek to alter this Democrat invention to its own needs. \par \par \uc2  However, Lindsey was consistent during the presidential campaign and since that the strong dollar policy had served the U.S. well and that it would be maintained under Bush. \par \par \uc2  Lindsey said the Bush administration is seeking to maintain a strong dollar through three core policies. \par \par \uc2  The first is support for an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its objective of encouraging strong economic growth with low inflation. \par \par \uc2  "The Federal Reserve has done an outstanding job in carrying out a non-inflationary monetary policy during the past two decades," Lindsey observed. \par \par \uc2  "In this regard, not only does the administration recognize the Fed's independence, it also shares and supports the Fed's objective of maximum sustainable growth with low inflation," he added. \par \par \uc2  The second policy pillar is ensuring the U.S. remains attractive to foreign investors by properly nurturing the business climate in America. This necessitates investments in education, the energy sector and an overhaul of the tax system, which have been hallmarks of the nascent Bush government, Lindsey argued. \par \par \uc2  "Prudent management of fiscal policy will also contribute to a strong economy and a strong dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  The third and final pillar supporting the dollar is the administration's commitment to "an open and robust trading environment." Accordingly, the Bush government will continue to guarantee the free flow of capital and goods in and out of the U.S., Lindsey said, while also pushing for other countries to follow its lead. \par \par \uc2  "The Bush administration recognizes that the benefits of having a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  aren't free, and they require careful and responsible attention to the formation and articulation of national economic policy," Lindsey said. \par \par \uc2  "However, the benefits of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for our country far outweigh the costs. Thus, a commitment to a stable, non-inflationary \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a good business environment, and open trade will remain the cornerstones of American economic policy, he concluded. \par \par \uc2  -By Damian Milverton, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9272; damian.milverton@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010710dx4a00n5u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
