\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen claws back losses after Japan gets tough. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2001\par 14:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 6 (Reuters) - The yen halved earlier one percent losses against the dollar on Friday after Japan reiterated it was not prepared to see its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  spiral lower. \par \par \uc2  Top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda said Japan would take action in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if current trends continued, adding to a drumbeat of warnings in the past two days that Japan would not tolerate further rapid declines in the yen's value against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were particularly wary of pushing the yen lower ahead of a meeting of Asian finance ministers in Kuala Lumpur at the weekend. \par \par \uc2  "The market is anxious about the prospect of yen supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Avinash Persaud, head of global research at State Street Bank. \par \par \uc2  "There is some concern Asian finance ministers will issue a statement after the meeting warning of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  further down the line." \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped to 124.40 yen from levels near 125.35 before Kuroda's comments, leaving it over two yen below two-and-a-half year highs set on Monday. The euro fell even faster, falling 1-1/2 yen to below 111.30, dragging it down against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The yen had earlier fallen sharply against both the dollar and the euro as the market gave a chilly reception to a long-awaited package of economic measures. \par \par \uc2  The package, aimed mainly at speeding up the disposals of bad loans by Japan's troubled banks, contained little that had not already been factored into the market and prompted a classic "buy on rumour, sell on fact" reaction, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  EURO SLIPS BACK \par \par \uc2  The euro, which outperformed the dollar early in the day, slipped back to day's lows around $0.8930 as dealers awaited key U.S. payrolls data at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The euro showed little reaction to Bank of France Governor Jean-Claude Trichet's reiteration of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "wait-and-see" stance on interest rates, which was balanced by more dovish comments that the upward risks to price stability had receded. \par \par \uc2  Trichet's comments are the latest in a series from top ECB officials keeping the markets guessing on the timing of an expected rate cut. \par \par \uc2  But a Reuters poll of 48 economists on Friday still showed a majority of economists still expected the ECB to cut rates at its next meeting on April 11. \par \par \uc2  The ECB is the only major \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to have cut interest rates this year, prompting concerns it is jeopardising euro zone growth prospects at a time of slowing global growth. \par \par \uc2  "Investors are betting that the United States was the first into recession and will be the first out of it," said State Street's Persaud. "This is supporting the dollar at a time when there are fears the ECB's intransigence risks leaving the euro zone behind." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  THREAT? \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made many forays into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent years and has ample firepower with over $360 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. But many analysts are sceptical \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen against the dollar at current levels. \par \par \uc2  "Kuroda seems to be talking tough to appease his Asian neighbours," said Russell Jones, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers. "In reality, Japan is more likely to be concerned with slowing the yen's fall rather than halting it." \par \par \uc2  Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns agreed: "Kuroda was saying pretty much the same as what he said yesterday and what Japanese officials have been saying all week." \par \par \uc2  Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party was also under the spotlight after Prime Minister Yoshiro Mori said he intended to resign, with elections for a new leader to be held on April 24. \par \par \uc2  Mori's resignation announcement had a muted impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets but dealers said focus would now turn to the LDP's election for a new leader. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4602m5k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
