\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen scoops gains as Japan officials get tough. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 784 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2001\par 14:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 5 (Reuters) - The yen bounced on Thursday, gaining two percent from recent 17-month lows against the euro and setting one-week highs against the dollar, as top Japanese officials said further rapid yen falls would not be tolerated. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said the basic principle among the Group of Seven industrial nations was to resist any rapid moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  His deputy, Haruhiko Kuroda, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements abated, while Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Masaru Hayami warned against an overly rapid fall in the yen against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The market is taking a lot of notice of these comments," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in London. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that all three have spoken is having the desired impact." \par \par \uc2  In addition, Japan's top financial bureaucrat Toshiro Muto said in European trading hours that moves in the dollar/yen rate since last week had been too rapid, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take appropriate action in the market as needed. \par \par \uc2  A senior official of the International Monetary Fund, Kunio Saito, also said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be warranted if there were excessive volatility in the dollar/yen exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had gained four percent in three trading days to 2-1/2 year highs of 126.82 yen by the end of last week, but had pared more than half of those gains by Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell two yen from the day's highs before rebounding half a yen from key 123.50 support by 1145 GMT, according to Reuters data. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to lows around 111.25 yen, down more than two yen from Wednesday's 17-month highs around 113.70, before recovering to around 112. \par \par \uc2  "The market has had a propensity to run very long of dollar/yen and euro/yen," said Graham Cocks, manager corporate treasury at Mellon Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "Anything that could upset things badly creates a bit of profit-taking." \par \par \uc2  Traders said the yen was also benefiting from a more than one percent rise in the Nikkei share average and expectations that an economic package to bolster Japan's flagging economy and stocks would be unveiled on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The fact these comments have come today means the market is likely to take more notice of any positive surprises from the package tomorrow," said Halpenny. \par \par \uc2  BOE CUTS RATES \par \par \uc2  Sterling took in its stride the Bank of England's decision to cut interest rates by 25 basis points to 5.5 percent, as markets had anticipated. \par \par \uc2  The BOE Monetary Policy Committee said it was concerned by a deteriorating global economic outlook, falling equity markets and the impact of the foot and mouth crisis. \par \par \uc2  The growth-boosting impact of a rate cut outweighed the reduction of the interest rate advantage the pound holds over the dollar, traders and analysts said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. interest rates currently stand at 5.00 percent. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was trading at $1.4363/73 at 1145 GMT, off early one-week highs around $1.44 but steady from levels seen shortly before the rate decision. \par \par \uc2  EURO VACILLATES \par \par \uc2  The euro see-sawed in a one-cent range, taking its cue against the dollar from moves in the euro/yen exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to two-week peaks near $0.91 early in Europe before retreating below the key $0.90 level. \par \par \uc2  A reversal in the dollar's fortunes pushed the euro above $0.90 earlier this week, after it hit 3-1/2-month lows around $0.8740 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Expectations the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , seen as a laggard among central banks in boosting growth, would finally cut interest rates next week was giving the euro some support, analysts said, particularly after a recent batch of weaker-than-expected euro zone data. \par \par \uc2  German manufacturing orders fell in February for the second month in row, data showed on Thursday, dropping 0.2 percent month-on-month against expectations of a rise. \par \par \uc2  ASIAN CONCERNS OVER YEN \par \par \uc2  The Mainichi Shimbun daily reported on Thursday that members of the Association of South East Asian Nations would voice their concern about the weakening yen in a joint statement to be issued after their meeting in Malaysia this weekend. \par \par \uc2  South Korea has already made known its worries over the effect a rapidly weakening yen could have on its own economy by making its exports less competitive. \par \par \uc2  China would find itself in a very uncomfortable situation should the yen fall to 130 or 140 against the dollar, Yu Yongding, director of a Chinese government think tank, was reported as saying in Tokyo on Thursday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4502kh4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
