\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen jumps on spate of Japan warnings. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 818 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2001\par 12:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Updates prices, adds fresh quotes, latest comments on yen, UK data, changes byline) \par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 5 (Reuters) - The yen rebounded two percent from recent 17-month lows against the euro and set one-week highs against the dollar on Thursday as top Japanese officials said further rapid yen falls would not be tolerated. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said in Asia the basic principle among the Group of Seven industrial nations was to resist any rapid moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  His deputy, Haruhiko Kuroda, said Tokyo was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements abated, while Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Masaru Hayami warned against an overly rapid fall in the yen against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The market is taking a lot of notice of these comments," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in London. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that all three have spoken is having the desired impact." \par \par \uc2  In addition, Japan's top financial bureaucrat Toshiro Muto said in European trading hours that moves in the dollar/yen rate since last week had been too rapid, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take appropriate action in the market as needed. \par \par \uc2  A senior official of the International Monetary Fund, Kunio Saito, also said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be warranted if there were excessive volatility in the dollar/yen exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  The dollar gained four percent in three trading days to 2-1/2 year highs of 126.82 yen by the end of last week, but had pared more than half of those gains by Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell two yen from the day's highs before bouncing slightly from key 123.50 support by 0935 GMT, according to Reuters data. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to lows around 111.25 yen, down more than two yen from Wednesday's 17-month highs around 113.70. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the yen was also benefiting from a more than one percent rise in the Nikkei share average and expectations that an economic package to bolster Japan's flagging economy and stocks would be unveiled on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The fact these comments have come today means the market is likely to take more notice of any positive surprises from the package tomorrow," said Halpenny. \par \par \uc2  EURO PULLS BACK \par \par \uc2  The euro's losses against the yen dragged it below $0.90 support, a cent below two-week highs set earlier in Europe. A reversal in the dollar's fortunes took the euro above $0.90 this week to peaks four percent above 3-1/2-month lows set on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Expectations the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , seen as a laggard in boosting growth, would finally cut interest rates next week was giving the euro some supoprt, analysts said, particularly after a batch of weaker-than-expected euro zone data on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The market is awaiting some move by the ECB at the next meeting and a lot of the euro zone economic indicators do warrant some action," Neil MacKinnon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  "The sooner they cut rates the better (for the euro zone growth and the euro)." \par \par \uc2  BLEAK YEN AHEAD \par \par \uc2  While the yen was buoyant on Thursday, analysts said its gains would be limited given Japan's economic and financial problems. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Kuroda had not made the ministry's task of managing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  any easier by publishing a newspaper article that sowed confusion in the market. \par \par \uc2  A day after saying the yen's recent moves had been a little too rapid, Kuroda wrote in the Asian Wall Street Journal (AWSJ) that the depreciation was in fact largely in line with fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Asked by reporters to clarify his comments, Kuroda said, "It is true that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves over the past one to two weeks have been quite rapid. If \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were to reflect fundamentals, the changes should be more gradual." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Mainichi Shimbun daily reported on Thursday that members of the Association of South East Asian Nations would voice their concern about the weakening yen in a joint statement to be issued after their meeting in Malaysia this weekend. \par \par \uc2  South Korea has already made known its worries over the effect a rapidly weakening yen could have on its own economy by making its exports less competitive. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, sterling hit one-week peaks against the dollar but fell to two-week troughs against the euro ahead of the Bank of England's interest rate decision at 1100 GMT. \par \par \uc2  In a Reuters poll, 18 economists out of 30 forecast a cut in the repo rate from 5.75 percent but a dozen saw no change. Overall, they gave a 58 percent likelihood of a cut. \par \par \uc2  Sterling dipped after data showing British industrial output fell 0.3 percent in February, against expectations of growth. Manufacturing output rose by 0.1 percent in February, following a slump of 0.8 percent in January. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4502k8z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
