\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  options back euro bullishness against dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2001\par 18:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 5 (Reuters) - The euro is outperforming the dollar after Japan warned it would not tolerate a rapid fall in the yen against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prices in the options market show this trend is expected to last at least a month. \par \par \uc2  Warnings that Japan would not tolerate a further rapid dollar rise are encouraging traders to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen rather than the euro, particularly given speculation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could deliver a growth-boosting interest rate cut as early as next week. \par \par \uc2  This has already seen the euro this week stage its sharpest rally against the dollar since early January, gaining three cents in as many days. \par \par \uc2  And investors are betting on more gains, given that for the first time in almost a month they are willing to pay a premium for euro call options, which give them the right to buy euros for dollars, over puts, which give them the right to sell euros. \par \par \uc2  "There has been a big change in sentiment towards the euro in a very short space of time," said Jesper Dannesboe, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein. \par \par \uc2  "The euro's hasty bounce from its lows is an encouraging sign and we expect the skew to remain in favour of euro calls for some time." \par \par \uc2  One-month options showed euro calls were favoured over euro puts against the dollar with a bias of 0.2 percentage points on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The bias had been 0.6 percentage points in favour of euro puts as recently as three days ago as the euro tumbled below $0.8750 to its lowest level this year. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN GIVES HELPING HAND \par \par \uc2  Murmurings of discontent about the dollar/yen rate at the start of the week grew louder and blunter on Thursday as Japanese Deputy Finance Minister Haruhiko Kuroda warned \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves abated. \par \par \uc2  Senior International Monetary Fund official Kunio Saito also said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was warranted if there was excessive volatility in the dollar/yen rate. \par \par \uc2  Japan has made many forays into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent years and, with over $360 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, it has real firepower. \par \par \uc2  "Euro buying against the yen has certainly given the euro a leg-up this week," said Ian Groom, options strategist at ABN AMRO Bank. \par \par \uc2  "The skew for euro puts was at its highest this year on Monday and the turnaround in risk reversals has been dramatic." \par \par \uc2  CHINK IN DOLLAR'S ARMOUR \par \par \uc2  This chink in the dollar's armour appeared just as euro bulls were despairing at the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's seeming invincibility to a spate of negative news. \par \par \uc2  Mounting evidence that the U.S. economy is slowing has done little to dent the appeal of the dollar, which has rallied over five percent against the euro since the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  Longer-term investors, however, seem harder to convince that the euro's dog days are over. \par \par \uc2  While one-month options have swung in favour of euro calls, longer dated options - which tend to be most in demand from corporates - are still giving a bearish reading. \par \par \uc2  "The euro's rebound above 90 cents brightened its short-term outlook but longer-term investors remain cautious," said Mark Henry, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at GNI. \par \par \uc2  "Investors have had their fingers burnt by the euro too many times to become overly optimistic." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4502k38\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
