\par\par \b \uc2  Japan rhetoric seen insufficient defence for yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2001\par 15:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 3 (Reuters) - Japan has protested against the speed of the yen's fall, but may have to follow its words with action to stop a decline which is causing its Asian neighbours to fret, analysts said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Japanese competitiveness has been boosted at a time when its economy badly needs help as the yen hit its lowest since 1998 against the dollar this week, shedding four percent in nearly as many trading days and 10 percent in the past month. \par \par \uc2  But the authorities are starting to flash go-slow signs at markets, not least because of the potential for friction with the United States and the concerns being voiced elsewhere in Asia as regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  get dragged down with the yen. \par \par \uc2  Japanese words have won the yen a reprieve for now but analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risked extending its losses, albeit at a slower pace, until Japan took a stronger stand. \par \par \uc2  "If the yen continues to depreciate at the speed it has been falling recently, it would cause grave concern in Asia," said Phil Rands, chief dealer at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not an issue at this stage but people see the yen falling further and if it goes to 135 or 140 this quarter we would see them trying to dampen down the market." \par \par \uc2  Japan has made many forays into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent years but was last spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the yen in the first half of 1998 as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was falling towards these levels. \par \par \uc2  The yen has already weakened as far as 126.82 against the dollar this week. \par \par \uc2  ASIA LESS QUIESCENT THAN UNITED STATES \par \par \uc2  The United States has so far kept mum about the yen's fall against the dollar, even though it comes against a backdrop of flagging U.S. growth, but Asian countries have been more vocal. \par \par \uc2  South Korea, whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has hit 30-month lows against the dollar, said on Tuesday it had expressed concern about the yen's decline to U.S. and Japanese authorities and was now discussing the issue with them. \par \par \uc2  Korean stocks and bonds have been hit by the weakness in the won, and it is also seen limiting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's room to cut its key short-term interest rate target this week. \par \par \uc2  China has also commented on the fallout from the yen's decline, with the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Dai Xianglong, predicting yen weakness would continue. \par \par \uc2  "I can say for sure the Japanese yen will devalue," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief was quoted by Business Week as saying. \par \par \uc2  "This will bring about successive devaluations in Southeast Asian countries and add to the pressure on the yuan." \par \par \uc2  Xianglong reiterated China's position that it would be able to hold its peg against the dollar regardless. \par \par \uc2  Against this backdrop, a chorus of Japanese voices, including that of Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, has in the past 48 hours warned dealers that they were moving too fast. \par \par \uc2  "Japan needs a weaker yen but they have realised that the move has been quicker than they would have liked," said Alfonso Prat-Gay, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at J.P. Morgan in London. \par \par \uc2  "The question is where does it become a policy problem and while we are starting to see the first elements of that, there is not yet enough consensus to do something about it." \par \par \uc2  That consensus could soon emerge, however, if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers pluck up their courage and drive the yen down anew. \par \par \uc2  While the barrage of Japanese comments has helped push the yen as much as one percent from its 2-1/2 year lows against the dollar, traders said there were plenty of triggers for a fresh run on the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  One could be if the economic package Japan is expected to finalise by Wednesday disappoints financial markets. \par \par \uc2  And if the yen starts falling too far, too fast, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have no option but to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's support. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is signalling this is not a one-way bet and that a 130 to 140 range would be unacceptable," said Stephen Saywell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in London. \par \par \uc2  "But if the rhetoric did not work and the market did push the limits of their tolerance, I would expect them to back their words up with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4302o8z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
