\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Yen pact talk swirls but target seen far-fetched. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 889 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 07:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 16 (Reuters) - The yen crumpled to a 20-month low in Tokyo on Friday as rumours swept markets that Japan and the United States had agreed to guide the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  even lower. \par \par \uc2  The yen slid to 122.73 yen to the dollar, its lowest level since July 1999 and a fall of around 17 percent from its peak around 101.30 last year. \par \par \uc2  And market chatter was that the world's two richest countries were prepared to see it fall another 15 percent or so to 140.00 to the dollar, largely as a last-ditch measure to shore up Japan's deteriorating economy. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts have long suspected that Japan would like to see some yen weakness as a boon to exports while the U.S. has an interest in maintaining a strong dollar policy to attract the foreign funds that fuel its massive trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  But they doubt either party would want to risk the yen falling as far as 140, both for domestic reasons and the adverse impact it could have on Japan's Asian neighbours. \par \par \uc2  LET'S TALK TARGETS \par \par \uc2  The latest rumours of a tacit target for dollar/yen emerged in New York on Thursday and, as if striving for extra credibility, specifically mentioned the official in charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), Haruhiko Kuroda, as the originator of the agreement. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda was in Washington for high-level meetings last week and dealers assume \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy must have been discussed. \par \par \uc2  But Kuroda strongly denied the talk on Friday, saying the two countries have never agreed to guide \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rates to certain levels. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also noted that only last Saturday Kuroda had stated that the MOF was opposed to deliberately weakening the yen and that he was confident the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not fall sharply, which a move to 140.00 would surely qualify as. \par \par \uc2  However, Japanese Trade Minister Takeo Hiranuma muddied the waters on Friday by welcoming the yen's fall to its current level of around 122.00 to the dollar as good for the economy. \par \par \uc2  He did go on to caution that a fall beyond 150.00 could scare foreign funds out of Japanese assets and should be prevented. \par \par \uc2  But choosing a level so far away from the yen's present position merely fed speculation that the authorities were prepared to see it weaken a lot further yet. \par \par \uc2  "If there's something ministers should learn about \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it's never mention a specific level. The market will only try and test it," advised a U.S. bank dealer in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "What made it worse in this case was that 150.00 is miles away. People assume that if there isn't a problem until 150.00, then it's OK to take the yen to, say, 140.00," he added. \par \par \uc2  He also noted that \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was completely in the hands of the MOF in Japan and the Trade Ministry had no say whatsoever, so Kuroda's standard line that the yen should move with fundamentals should be taken as the country's stance overall. \par \par \uc2  That did not stop the local media from detecting signs of a conspiracy to aggressively weaken the yen through active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not only by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  but the U.S. as well. \par \par \uc2  This particular idea was dismissed as "lunacy" by analysts given the States' avowed opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in any form and the risks of increased trade friction stemming from its massive current account deficit with Japan. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN'S WORST NIGHTMARE \par \par \uc2  Analysts also reckoned the Japanese authorities would be well aware that if foreign investors were to even scent that Japan was prepared to let the yen fall as far as 140.00 it could send them scrambling out of Japanese assets to avoid \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses. \par \par \uc2  Japanese equities are already under intense pressure, the stock market's benchmark Nikkei average having sunk to 16-year lows this week, and the last thing officials want is for Japanese government bonds (JGBs) to slide as well, which would almost certainly result if foreign investors fled. \par \par \uc2  "It's their worst nightmare - a triple selloff of the Nikkei, JGBs and the yen," said James Malcolm, an economist at J.P. Morgan. "No way they'd risk that by discussing targets like 140.00, even in a locked room with the music up and in a whisper." \par \par \uc2  Japan's authorities are also aware that the Asian neighbours would be none too happy with a deliberate policy of weakening the yen since they compete in many export markets. \par \par \uc2  With global growth slowing, Japan could only expand its exports by grabbing market share and that would be largely at the expense of countries such as South Korea and Taiwan. \par \par \uc2  That is why regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tend to track the yen ever lower in a damaging dance of trade competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  And then there is China. \par \par \uc2  "When the MOF first began talking down the yen in January there were rumours that China had played hell, had threatened to devalue the yuan if the yen fell much further," said the U.S. bank trader. \par \par \uc2  "There's no reason to think that China would be any happier now, and can you imagine the carnage in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  if that happened?" \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx3g03za3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
