\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro lags as dollar takes heart from jobs data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Javier David \par \uc2 872 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 01:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, March 9 (Reuters) - Impressively resilient U.S. employment data undermined the euro on Friday, which marginally recovered from an early 1/2 cent selloff against the dollar to spend most of the session encased in a narrow range. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also received little stimulus from investors whom depredated technology and blue-chip stocks, interpreting the U.S. labor market's buoyancy as a sign that aggressive interest rate cuts from the Federal Reserve would not be forthcoming. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that in spite of sharply lower U.S. equities, the euro fell prey to an all too common phenomenon which occurs before a weekend - market participants unloading the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in fear of their positions moving against them. \par \par \uc2  "When Europe was going home (the euro) got hammered," said a trader at one New York bank. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people expected the euro to climb up because the stock market is not doing very well, but it's not happening," he stated, indicating large selling interest near the 93.40/50 cents level. \par \par \uc2  In late U.S. dealings, the euro bought about 93.20 cents , virtually unchanged from its previous U.S. close but off the session's low beneath 93 cents. It traded half a cent from the high it struck during London's trading session. \par \par \uc2  Against a broadly firmer Japanese yen, Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  changed hands near 111.40 yen , 0.2 percent lower on the day and well off the session's high close to 112 yen. \par \par \uc2  JOBS DATA QUESTIONS US/EU GROWTH DIFFERENTIALS \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the Labor Department reported February nonfarm payrolls rose by 135,000, more than double Wall Street economists' consensus forecast of 62,000. The unemployment rate remained unchanged at 4.2 percent, on target with forecasts. \par \par \uc2  The jobs data softened the view that the euro zone economy will significantly outpace that of the United States this year. It also lowered expectations for an aggressive rate cut at the next Federal Reserve meeting, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  However, the reduced expectations for a Fed easing - along with an earnings warning from semiconductor giant Intel Corp. - led to a sharp drop in U.S. stock prices. \par \par \uc2  "What we've been seeing is expectations being built up that there's going to be a massive growth differential between the United States and Europe this year," said Greg Anderson, financial economist at Fleet Global Markets. "(But) this number confirms the strength of the U.S. economy." \par \par \uc2  JAPAN REMAINS IN POLITICAL FOG \par \par \uc2  While the U.S. economic data bolstered the dollar against the euro, the Japanese yen managed to hold its early gains, having backed away from the 20-month lows reached on Thursday, when it hit 120.40 yen per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stuck to a narrow 1/2 yen range for most of the U.S. session, and in late dealings changed hands near 119.50 yen , also about 0.20 percent lower than its previous close. \par \par \uc2  The yen got a lift earlier from newspaper reports that Japan's unpopular Prime Minister Yoshiro Mori was about to step down, although Jiji news agency later said the prime minister denied such reports. \par \par \uc2  Although analysts have previously said deposing Mori would be yen-positive, traders said any such event as a temporary palliative, saying the market saw precious little light at the end of the yen's obscured tunnel. \par \par \uc2  "Every day there's some new comment out of Japan, but it doesn't really matter anymore," said Bernard Tsui, vice president and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  advisor at Union Bank of California. \par \par \uc2  "Once the dust settles, Japan's economic fundamentals are pretty weak, (and they) are pointing to a weaker yen," he added. \par \par \uc2  Traders said markets saw little react from Haruhiko Kuroda's comments to U.S. and Japanese business leaders in New York. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said while it was not appropriate for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to deliberately weaken yen to boost exports, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be justified if the yen was not aligned with economic fundamentals. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 2239GMT. All data taken from Reuters calculated from the levels at 4:30 p.m. (2130 GMT) in the previous New York session. \par \par \uc2  US Close US Close  %Chg   YTD %  2000 Cls \par \par \uc2  09 Mar.  08 Mar. \par \par \uc2  ------------------------------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr\tab \tab  0.9327   0.9322   +0.05   -1.06   0.9422\par Dlr/yen\tab \tab   119.52   119.69   -0.14   +4.74   114.27\par Euro/yen\tab \tab  111.43   111.53   -0.09   +3.58   107.68\par Dlr/swiss\tab \tab 1.6518   1.6522   -0.02   +2.62   1.6100\par Stg/dlr\tab \tab   1.4692   1.4688   +0.03   -1.80   1.4957\par Dlr/can\tab \tab   1.5468   1.5452   +0.10   +3.13   1.4983\par Aus/dlr\tab \tab   0.5088   0.5109   -0.41   -8.52   0.5585\par Euro/swiss\tab      1.5413   1.5402   +0.07   +1.28   1.5207\par Euro/stg\tab \tab  0.6344   0.6341   +0.05   +0.70   0.6297\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All spots Tokyo spots Europe spots Volatilities Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economic Forecasts...\tab     Official