\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Out of Japan's  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  chaos, a weaker yen will come. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 1,043 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 06:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 9 (Reuters) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is in disarray. \par \par \uc2  Not a day passes without one policy maker or another making a pronouncement on the yen only to backtrack later or be flatly contradicted by a colleague. \par \par \uc2  As a result, the yen has plunged to 20-month lows on the dollar, bounced and plunged again leaving dealers and analysts bemused but also overwhelmingly bearish. \par \par \uc2  "Often you get an outbreak of comments like this at a crisis point in an economy. It's a signal that things are falling apart and no one wants to take the blame," said Ken Landon, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  "And because it's a crisis signal, it's also a damn good reason for selling the yen," he added. \par \par \uc2  The only surprise was that the yen had not fallen further and faster, but herein lies a tale about the nature of the market itself. \par \par \uc2  WHAT A WEEK \par \par \uc2  The drama - perhaps mystery would be a better description - began on Monday when Bank of Japan Policy Board member Kazuo Ueda suggested the most effective form of quantitative easing would be to drive the yen lower through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Since \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is solely under the control of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), analysts saw this as an attempt to pass the buck by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is reluctant to take its own quantitative steps. \par \par \uc2  But with the yen falling fast by then, both the MOF and the Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry (METI) sounded notes of caution on Tuesday saying they were watching the market carefully. \par \par \uc2  And the spat over \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  further reflects the disarray betwen the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which have been blaming each other for mismanaging the economy. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday saw the BOJ re-enter the fray with Governor Masaru Hayami mentioning in a speech that a deliberate, massive decline in the yen was a possible policy option, though a drastic one. He later clarified his comments to say that the bank did not favour such a policy, but dealers wondered why he ever brought it up given the market's sensitivity to such talk. \par \par \uc2  That same day, Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa also ruled out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to depress the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but suggested \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not object if it declined "naturally." \par \par \uc2  The plot thickened on Thursday when Miyazawa said Japan's finances were close to collapse, a slip that some saw as an indirect attempt to drag the yen lower. \par \par \uc2  Later the same day, Hayami sent traders' heads spinning by declaring that a strong yen was in Japan's interests, pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. \par \par \uc2  Farce was the theme on Friday with METI minister Takeo Hiranuma saying a lower yen was tolerable as long as it did not overshoot. Only to be immediately contradicted by Economics Minister Taro Aso, who declared that no country should welcome a fall in its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But this is not the only contradiction. \par \par \uc2  Officials are fond of saying the yen's value should reflect fundamentals. But the same officials cannot but concede that Japan's economic fundamentals are dire and growing worse by the day. So by their argument, the yen should be weakening. \par \par \uc2  And since it is mainly the MOF that emphasises fundamentals and the MOF that controls \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts reason that this is the policy that markets should focus on. \par \par \uc2  "It's the MOF that matters and for them fundamentals clearly argue for a lower \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Marshall Gittler, head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "Japan has no option in the short term. It's already forced all the other policy levers so far to the floor that they've broken off," he argued. \par \par \uc2  NATURE OF THE BEAST \par \par \uc2  Then again, some analysts wonder why the market should care what the authorities think given Japan's self-professed problems and its limited room for policy manoeuvre. \par \par \uc2  This is especially true for the BOJ since it has no control over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and, given the parlous state of the economy, hiking rates to defend the yen would be little short of suicide. \par \par \uc2  The market does have more to fear from the MOF since it has massive \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $363 billion and in the past has not been shy in using them to affect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But again, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lift the yen would be tantamount to a tightening of policy, something hard to justify with the economy near recession. \par \par \uc2  And in any case the vast, trillion dollar-a-day \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has often taken on countries when their fundamentals were so clearly calling for weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It did not hesitate to drive the yen lower during the Asian crisis in 1997 and 1998, taking it to 147 to the dollar despite determined \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the MOF. \par \par \uc2  But something happened in late 1998 that changed the nature of the beast, perhaps for good. \par \par \uc2  When the Russian crisis broke on the world it caught the market's major speculators in one of the biggest bear traps in history. As investors turned risk-averse to an unimaginable degree, huge bets made on spread convergence blew up, bringing down the biggest speculator of all: Long Term Capital Management. \par \par \uc2  And one of the biggest bets of all was against the yen. Fund managers and investment banks across the globe had funded their investments by borrowing at low rates in yen to lend at fatter yields abroad. \par \par \uc2  With the Russian crisis and the fall of LTCM, the wager went horribly wrong and the yen soared from 145 to the dollar in late August to 111 a month later, at one point gaining 18 percent in a single week. \par \par \uc2  This singular event left speculators deeply scarred. Many of the large macro hedge funds have closed shop or turned to less risky investments, while investment banks are loath to leverage credit or risk propriety positions as they did in the past. \par \par \uc2  "The market's just not the same. If those guys were still around you can bet the yen would be a lot weaker right now," said Deutsche's Landon. \par \par \uc2  It is like an ocean without sharks, safer for the vulnerable but lacking the visceral excitement of old. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx39045ol\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
