\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen gets fillip from Hayami, dlr suffers most. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 701 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 14:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Updates prices with fresh quotes, adds details of BOE rate decision) \par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 8 (Reuters) - The yen more than reversed early losses against the dollar on Thursday after remarks from the Bank of Japan's governor sparked a rebound from 20-month lows reached on earlier comments by the Japanese finance minister. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded almost one percent after BOJ governor Masaru Hayami said a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in Japan's interests and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep sterilising liquidity stemming from any potential \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen had earlier fallen to its weakest since July 1999 against the dollar after Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said Japan's finances were near collapse and fundamental fiscal reform was needed. \par \par \uc2  "The yen has been choppy on the back of Hayami's and Miyazawa's comments," said a trader at a Japanese bank in London. "Hayami has put a cautious tone on the market's yen bearishness but the overall picture has not changed because even if what he says makes sense, people will be looking at what the economy is doing." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was the worst performer amongst the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the euro was supported by data showing euro zone growth outstripped that of the United States while sterling reaped the rewards of Wednesday's well-received budget. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit peaks around 120.40 yen, according to Reuters data, before retreating more than a yen to hit the day's lows around 119.30 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro earlier approached 112 yen, a little below Wednesday's seven-week highs, before easing and settling near the middle of the day's range around 111.50. \par \par \uc2  The yen was still one percent below this week's highs against the dollar as weak economic data continued to paint a gloomy picture of the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Japanese machinery orders data showed a 11.8 percent drop in January from the previous month, far worse than the 6.1 percent fall analysts had been expecting. \par \par \uc2  Japanese politics are practically paralysed as the ruling party prepares to oust the unpopular Yoshiro Mori and replace him as prime minister with someone more electorally viable. \par \par \uc2  Traders said on a longer-term view, Miyazawa's remarks were likely to carry more weight than Hayami's comments. Miyazawa said on Wednesday that a weaker yen was fine as long as it happened naturally and this contributed to its recent weakness. \par \par \uc2  "Miyazawa is in charge of whatever \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  does in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the degrees of freedom Japanese policy-makers have are very small - a weaker yen remains the most plausible option," said Ryan Shea, international economist at Bank One. \par \par \uc2  "His comments are a backdoor method of weakening the yen." \par \par \uc2  BOE HOLDS RATES \par \par \uc2  The dollar weakened against the euro and shed more than one cent against sterling. The pound benefited from the perception that the British budget was prudent enough to allow more stimulative interest rate reductions to underpin an already robust economy. \par \par \uc2  While the Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee, as expected, left rates on hold at 5.75 percent earlier on Thursday, traders expected rate cuts in coming months. \par \par \uc2  "The decision to leave rates unchanged was very much expected, but another rate cut is still likely in April or May," said Stephen Hannah, head of market economics Europe at National Australia Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "The background for sterling is still positive with the UK economy proving resilient and the budget should underpin growth." \par \par \uc2  Sterling held near the day's highs around $1.4700 after the rate decision. \par \par \uc2  The euro hit peaks around $0.9340, finding further support from data showing that the euro zone economy grew 0.7 percent in the fourth quarter, outstripping a U.S. rise of 0.3 percent in the same period. \par \par \uc2  A$ HITS RECORD LOW \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Australian dollar hit record lows around US$0.5070, undermined by Wednesday's data showing the Australian economy contracted in the December quarter. \par \par \uc2  Traders were also disappointed with the Reserve Bank of Australia's quarter percentage point rate cut a day ago, as it was seen too small to improve growth prospects. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx3804q9v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
