\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen lifted off 20-month lows by BOJ's Hayami. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 12:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 8 (Reuters) - The yen more than reversed its early losses against the dollar on Thursday after remarks from the Bank of Japan's governor sparked a rebound from 20-month lows hit earlier on comments by the Japanese finance minister. \par \par \uc2  The yen fell to its weakest level since July 1999 against the dollar after Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said the country's finances were near collapse and fundamental fiscal reform was needed. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded almost one percent from earlier lows after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Masaru Hayami said a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in Japan's interests and the BOJ would keep sterilising liquidity stemming from any potential \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The yen has been choppy on the back of Hayami's and Miyazawa's comments," said a trader at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "Hayami has put a cautious tone on the market's yen bearishness but the overall picture has not changed because even if what he says makes sense, people will be looking at what the economy is doing." \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose as far as 120.40 yen, according to Reuters data, before retreating more than a yen to hit day's lows around 119.30 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro in Asian trade approached 112 yen, a little below Wednesday's seven-week highs, before easing and settling near the middle of the day's range around 111.50 in European trades. \par \par \uc2  The yen was still one percent below this week's highs against the dollar as weak economic data continued to paint a gloomy picture about the health of the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday Japanese machinery orders data showed a 11.8 percent drop in January from the previous month, far worse than the 6.1 percent fall analysts had been expecting. \par \par \uc2  Japanese politics are practically paralysed as the ruling party prepares to oust the unpopular Yoshiro Mori and replace him as prime minister with someone more electorally viable. \par \par \uc2  Traders said on a longer-term view, Miyazawa's remarks were likely to carry more weight than Hayami's comments. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa's remarks on Wednesday that a weaker yen was fine as long as it happened naturally contributed to recent yen weakness. \par \par \uc2  "Miyazawa is in charge of whatever \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  does in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the degrees of freedom Japanese policy-makers have are very small - a weaker yen remains the most plausible option," said Ryan Shea, international economist at Bank One. \par \par \uc2  "His comments are a backdoor method of weakening the yen." \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR FALLS VS EURO, STERLING \par \par \uc2  The dollar, meanwhile, weakened against the euro as it shed a full cent against sterling. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was benefiting from the perception that the British budget, unveiled on Wednesday, was prudent enough to allow more stimulative interest rate reductions, underpinning an already robust economy. \par \par \uc2  However, most economists expected the Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee to leave rates on hold at 5.75 percent, following its regular two-day monthly meeting that ends on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro held near the day's highs around $0.9335 as sterling rose to hit peaks around $1.4695. \par \par \uc2  The euro held near the day's highs after provisional euro zone GDP data showed a 0.7 percent rise in fourth quarter 2000 growth, taking annual growth to 3.0 percent, slightly below forecasts. \par \par \uc2  German Finance Minister Hans Eichel forecast Europe would grow faster than the United States this year. \par \par \uc2  A$ HITS RECORD LOW \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Australian dollar fell to record lows against the U.S. dollar, undermined by Wednesday's data showing the Australian economy had shifted into reverse in the December quarter. \par \par \uc2  Traders were also disappointed with the Reserve Bank of Australia's quarter percentage point rate cut a day ago, as it was not seen too small to improve growth prospects. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar fell to a low of US$0.5067 in early European trade, surpassing the previous record low set in late November. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx3804nx4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
