\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen under pressure, 120 against dollar beckons. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 710 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 01:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 7 (Reuters) - The yen weakened on Wednesday, dropping to six-week lows against the euro and within reach of 20-month lows near 120 against the dollar amid confusion over the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's attitude toward yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The latest yen selloff picked up when Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Masaru Hayami mentioned in a speech that one drastic policy option put to the bank had been to substantially weaken the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Hayami went on to list problems with such a policy and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later denied Hayami had ever implied the bank would actually use \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a means of easing policy. But that did nothing to lessen traders' surprise that he had brought it up at all given his longstanding defence of a strong yen. \par \par \uc2  "What it does suggest is that Hayami is becoming more concerned about the domestic problems and the market is sensing that there is more easing to come," said Stephen Saywell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Citibank in London. \par \par \uc2  "It does appear that both the Finance Ministry and the Bank of Japan are now more willing to accept a weaker yen because of the lack of flexibility in their monetary and fiscal policies." \par \par \uc2  Japanese interest rates are already near zero, and repeated fiscal pump-priming measures over the past decade have left the government with a huge debt mountain. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose by a percent on the day toward 119.70 yen , within shouting distance of Monday's six-week highs around 119.75 and January's 20-month highs, just short of the crucial 120-yen milestone. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to seven-week highs near 112 yen , outpacing the dollar amid prevailing optimism on growth outlook in the euro zone relative to that in the United States. \par \par \uc2  CONFUSION OVER BOJ POLICY \par \par \uc2  The confusion over the BOJ's intentions follows last week's interest rate cut, an awkward softening in the bank's policy stance, given it had raised rates only last August. \par \par \uc2  Since the latest rate cut, the yen has been under pressure and the market has become nervous about a further wavering in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy. \par \par \uc2  "He shouldn't have mentioned it at all; even as a possible, maybe, perhaps option," a U.S. bank dealer in Tokyo said of Hayami's remarks on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "If it's an option to the bank, no matter how unlikely, then it's a risk to the market. It's going to hang over the yen for weeks to come." \par \par \uc2  Hayami's recent comments that more structural reform from the government was necessary to make monetary policy effective was also seen as a sticking point, given Japanese politics were in complete disarray at present. \par \par \uc2  "What the market needs is the government leading reform, and this is hardly visible," said Karl Broeker, head of Treasury at Landesbank Baden-Wuerrtenburg in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the political scene came near to farce on Wednesday as the domestic media carried report after report claiming Japan's unloved Prime Minister Yoshiro Mori had resolved to announce his resignation ahead of an LDP conference on March 13. \par \par \uc2  Mori angrily denied every one, saying he had absolutely no intention of resigning. \par \par \uc2  EURO KEEPS LOW PROFILE, AWAITS U.S. DATA \par \par \uc2  The euro was relatively steady against the dollar, although it briefly edged up toward Monday's three-week highs around $0.9380 on the back of heavy euro/yen buying in the wake of Hayami comments. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market was awaiting key U.S. economic reports, including the Federal Reserve's Beige Book due out at 1900 GMT and Friday's February employment report. \par \par \uc2  German manufacturing orders for January were also due out at 1030 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, sterling drifted in a tight range ahead of British Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown's Budget statement later in the day. \par \par \uc2  A big mover on the day was the Australian dollar, which fell more than a percent on the day toward US$0.5120 after the Reserve Bank of Australia cut interest rates by 0.25 point to 5.50 percent. It was also sideswiped by a shock 0.6 percent drop in Australia's fourth-quarter GDP. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx3704q81\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
