\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen skids to 20-month lows past 120 per dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 12:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 7 (Reuters) - The yen fell beyond 120 to the dollar for the first time in 20-months on Wednesday as expectation grew that Japan would tolerate a weaker yen to bolster its flagging economy. \par \par \uc2  Comments from Japan's Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa that it would be acceptable for the yen to weaken naturally gave the dollar the spur to rise above the 120 barrier - a level that has frustrated it on numerous forays higher in recent months. \par \par \uc2  Yen traders had already endured a whipsaw session after earlier comments from Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami that one option put to the bank had been to weaken the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The bank later denied Hayami had implied such a move was being considered. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese policymakers seem to be giving the green light to further yen weakness," said Jeremy Hawkins, economic adviser at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "Miyazawa seems to be giving a weaker yen the nod and the fact Hayami actually raised the issue of a weaker yen policy, even if to highlight the risks, would suggest a shift in policy." \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rise above the 120-yen milestone also lifted the euro above 112 yen - its highest since mid-January. \par \par \uc2  Japanese interest rates are already near zero, and repeated fiscal pump-priming measures over the past decade have left the government with a huge debt mountain. \par \par \uc2  WEAKER YEN OPTION? \par \par \uc2  Confusion reigned over the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's attitude toward yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Hayami's comments, despite a denial by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that Hayami had ever implied the bank would actually use \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a means of easing policy. \par \par \uc2  But that did nothing to lessen traders' surprise that he had raised the subject, given his long-standing defence of a strong yen. \par \par \uc2  "What it does suggest is that Hayami is becoming more concerned about the domestic problems and the market is sensing that there is more easing to come," said Stephen Saywell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Citibank in London. \par \par \uc2  "It does appear that both the Finance Ministry and the Bank of Japan are now more willing to accept a weaker yen because of the lack of flexibility in their monetary and fiscal policies." \par \par \uc2  The confusion over the BOJ's intentions follows last week's interest rate cut - an awkward softening in the bank's policy stance after it raised rates only last August. \par \par \uc2  Since the latest rate cut the yen has been under pressure, with dealers nervous about a further wavering in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy. \par \par \uc2  EURO KEEPS LOW PROFILE, AWAITS U.S. DATA \par \par \uc2  The euro held steady against the dollar, although it briefly edged up toward Monday's three-week highs around $0.9380 on the back of heavy euro/yen buying in the wake of Hayami comments. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market was awaiting key U.S. economic reports, including the Federal Reserve's Beige Book due at 1900 GMT and Friday's February employment report. \par \par \uc2  The euro showed little reaction to data showing a 0.1 percent rise in euro zone producer prices in January and a fall in German manufacturing orders in the same month. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, sterling drifted in a tight range ahead of British Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown's Budget statement later in the day. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar appeared on target to test last November's record low as growth plunged into negative territory for the first time since the recession of the early 1990s. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar fell more than one percent on the day to US$0.5120 , hit by data showing a shock 0.6 percent drop in Australia's fourth-quarter GDP and a quarter-point interest rate cut. \par \par \uc2  On the crosses, the Aussie was at 26-month lows against the euro, 10-month lows against the kiwi and 3-1/2 month lows against the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx3704lpp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
