\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Advances Against Euro, Sets New High for the Year Against Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 885 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 14:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  A WSJ.com News Roundup \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar rose to a nearly 20-month high against the yen Wednesday after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governor suggested \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen was an option. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials later sought to clarify that Governor Masuru Hayami was speaking only in general terms and was actually trying to cast doubt on such a plan. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also rose against the euro, helped in part by more disappointing German economic data. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the dollar traded at 120.03 yen, up from 118.88 yen late Tuesday in New York. The dollar hit an intraday high of 120.24 yen in European trading, a new high for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year. \par \par \uc2  The euro bought 92.96 U.S. cents, down from 93.47 U.S. cents late Tuesday. The pound fetched $1.4596, down from $1.4675 late Tuesday. The Bank of England will conclude a two-day policy-setting meeting Thursday. It is widely expected that the bank will make no change in interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Hayami said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen was one option policy makers could conceivably consider to bolster the slumping Japanese economy. "From the standpoint of drastic measures that could be tried, guiding the yen sharply lower through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one thing that is conceivable," Mr. Hayami said. \par \par \uc2  A weaker Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps the nation's exporters by making their products cheaper abroad and boosting the value of repatriated earnings. \par \par \uc2  The dollar jumped more than half a yen immediately after Mr. Hayami's remark was reported. The dollar has been struggling for several days to breach the 120-yen level. \par \par \uc2  A bank official later said the comment was taken out of context and pointed out that Mr. Hayami quickly qualified his remark by saying that such a policy would carry great risks. \par \par \uc2  "We have to be cautious considering it's unclear to what extent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates can be controlled through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to what extent [a weaker yen] would increase import costs and adversely affect the rest of Asia," Mr. Hayami said, according to a transcript of the speech. \par \par \uc2  "Regardless of these qualifying remarks from the BOJ and Hayami, [the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor's] remarks are a green light to overseas players to buy the dollar-yen," said Shuji Takano, assistant \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at ABN Amro Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  However, other analysts said the market overreacted to Mr. Hayami's comments. \par \par \uc2  "I think the market took them [Mr. Hayami's comments] more at face value than they were. I think he was talking hypothetically," said Robert Sinche, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  "The more important point on Hayami's comments is on a longer-term basis, in that the BOJ is thinking about more radical policy adjustments," Mr. Sinche said. "A return to a zero-interest rate policy is unlikely to be their last move. They're likely to consider, and adopt, more aggressive procedures that would inject significant liquidity into the Japanese economy," he said, adding that those procedures might include inflation targeting and monetary targeting. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said that while he had no intention of deliberately weakening the yen as a means of fighting deflation, he didn't consider it a problem if the yen weakens by itself. \par \par \uc2  "I don't intend to follow such a policy," Mr. Miyazawa said when asked whether the ministry would guide the yen lower as a means of fighting deflation. However, "if the yen weakens naturally then I don't see that there is a problem," he said. \par \par \uc2  The European common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  came under pressure after the release of worse-than-expected German manufacturing figures for January. The volume of German manufacturing industry orders fell a seasonally adjusted 3.9% in January but rose 9.7% on the year. Analysts had expected a 1% decline on the month and a 12.5% rise on the year in adjusted terms. \par \par \uc2  In London, Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown presented to Parliament a budget for the fiscal year ending March 2002 that assumes growth of between 2.25% and 2.75% in 2001 and inflation of 2.25%. The growth estimate is unchanged from the government's previous figure, while the inflation number is down from 2.5% previously. Mr. Brown said the budget surplus for the current fiscal year is forecast at 16.4 billion pounds, down a bit from the previously forecast 16.5 billion pounds. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the dollar came close to an all-time high against the Australian dollar. The Australian dollar dropped sharply to 50.9 U.S. cents during morning trading in New York, then stayed steady at that level the remainder of the session. The all-time low for the Australian dollar was set last November at 50.7 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline followed the Reserve Bank of Australia's decision to cut its overnight cash rate by one-quarter percentage point to 5.5%. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has now cut rates by 0.75 percentage point in the past two months to offset a potential recession. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020010711dx37008d5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
