\par\par \b \uc2  Japan MOF Kuroda: No Change In Japan's  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Policy \b0\par\par\uc2 160 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2001\par 01:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The Japanese government's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy remains unchanged, the vice minister for international affairs at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Haruhiko Kuroda, said Friday. \par \par \uc2  When asked by reporters about the dollar's fall from near Y120 to the Y117s late Thursday, Kuroda said, "As I've always reiterated, our \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is unchanged." \par \par \uc2  Kuroda also said that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should reflect fundamentals and should move in a stable manner. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy would change under U.S. Treasury Secretary-designate Paul O'Neill, Kuroda said there would be no change in the policy. \par \par \uc2  At 0040 GMT (7:40 p.m. EST Thursday), the dollar was trading at Y117.87, down from Y118.07 in New York late Thursday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2942;\par ron.harui@dowjones.com\par -0- 19/01/01 00-44G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx1j009yv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
