\par\par \b \uc2  Traders hesitate over driving dollar above 120 yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2001\par 14:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 18 (Reuters) - The U.S. Treasury Secretary-designate may have given them the green light, but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are reluctant to drive the dollar much above 120 yen just yet. \par \par \uc2  Paul O'Neill, nominee for the top job at the U.S. Treasury, endorsed Washington's long-standing commitment to a strong dollar on Wednesday, sparking a rally in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which took it to 18-month highs close to 120 yen on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  O'Neill's comments threw the focus back on the faltering Japanese economy, particularly in the absence of loud opposition to an export-boosting weaker yen by Japanese officials. \par \par \uc2  But hints of Japanese disquiet about their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 10-yen decline against the dollar in the past two months may be enough to slow the pace of the yen's losses beyond the psychological 120 level, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "We are starting to see some policymakers showing resistance to a weaker yen," said David Bloom, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  "We are hearing mutterings. If we get to 120, the mutterings will become a chorus, and above 120 yen they will become a cacophony." \par \par \uc2  The dollar retreated below 119 yen in European trade on Thursday after Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami indicated he regarded the yen's recent declines against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the result of a temporary shift in capital. \par \par \uc2  Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Zembei Mizoguchi said on Thursday that the ministry was watching \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements carefully, while Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said earlier this week that he was closely watching the market. \par \par \uc2  WEAK YEN - GOOD OR BAD? \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the benefit to Japanese exporters of a weaker yen was one of the few tools at Japan's disposal to prop up its ailing economy, since interest rates were already extremely low, and government debt levels high, limiting scope for fiscal stimulus. \par \par \uc2  But the negative impact of a weaker yen on domestic consumer confidence and on neighbouring economies in southeast Asia would make Japan reluctant to let the yen slide too far, they added. \par \par \uc2  "A weaker yen hits Japanese consumer confidence," said Gerard Lyons, global head of treasury reseach at Standard Chartered. "Exports are not the driving force of the economy everyone thinks they are." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has an active record of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from wild swings. \par \par \uc2  It \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen between April and June 1998, culminating with joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the United States when the yen crumpled below 144 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Between January 1999 and April 2000, Japanese authorities went the other way and sold yen at least 18 times, usually single-handedly, to prevent the yen from becoming too strong against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  This has made traders nervous of any large moves in the yen exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  MONEY COMING IN \par \par \uc2  The yen may also get a boost from repatriation of foreign assets by Japanese investors ahead of the annual book-closing in March, particularly if the Japanese stock market remains weak. \par \par \uc2  "The possibility of repatriation is always there, even though equity markets are looking healthier at the moment in Japan and the United States," said Glenn Davies, chief economist at Credit Lyonnais in London. \par \par \uc2  "If Japanese stock losses get more severe, Japanese investors could be forced to repatriate." \par \par \uc2  The decline in the Nikkei to a 27-month closing low this month was seen to have wiped out latent profit in the domestic stock portfolios at many Japanese institutions, notably life insurance firms, many of which are desperate to eke out cash to pay out dividend to policyholders. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo stocks gained for a fifth straight session on Thursday, but analysts said the gains may prove shortlived, as sentiment was artifically buoyed by a newly assembled ruling party task force discussing ways to prop up stock prices. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx1i01cv6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
