\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Watch: More Easing Of Dubious Merit, Economists Say \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nick Shindo \par \uc2 858 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2001\par 03:05\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Think the Bank of Japan should cut rates to spur demand? Think again, economists say. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stood fast in recent weeks despite increasing pressure from politicians who say the BOJ should revert back to the zero-rate policy it ended five months ago. \par \par \uc2  With stock prices in a tailspin and economic indicators pointing to an increasingly grim outlook, ruling Liberal Democratic party politicians and other critics say the nation needs radical easing steps to jolt private demand back to life. One option is for the BOJ to print money or increase purchases of government bonds, flooding the economy with money, they argue. \par \par \uc2  Many economists support the BOJ's resolve to ignore this static interference. For one thing, they argue, monetary conditions are already so slack that attempts at further easing wouldn't make much difference. The BOJ pumped a massive Y46.5 trillion into money markets towards the end of last year - far more than it did at the end of 1999 to prepare for the anticipated Y2K-related liquidity crunch that never came. \par \par \uc2  And even if the financial market gets more money, it wouldn't help the economy much while financial institutions are still saddled with huge amounts of debt, economists say. Preoccupied with reducing their liabilities, financial firms have been reluctant to lend to all but the most creditworthy borrowers. \par \par \uc2  Economists also have doubts about proposals calling for steps aimed at increasing demand by sparking "healthy" inflation. Proponents say such steps, which could be achieved by the BOJ underwriting government bond issues, would revive consumption. \par \par \uc2  But economists warn that such steps would be either ineffective or dangerous, as they could lead to runaway inflation. \par \par \uc2  "This talk that the BOJ should embark on an experimental monetary policy and change the way it does \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  is all nonsense," said Jesper Koll, chief economist at Merrill Lynch in Tokyo. "There is no crisis and there are no alarm bells ringing." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Weak Yen Lets BOJ Buy Time\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the fact that critics continue to call on the BOJ to take radical\par steps shows what a bind the Japanese economy is in. While other\par industrialized countries have managed to steer their economies largely\par through monetary policy, Japan hasn't been able to achieve a solid\par recovery even after spending tens of trillions of yen in fiscal outlays\par and despite keeping rates ultra-low for years.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Unfortunately for Japan, there's no panacea for the economy in the\par form of an innovative monetary measure, economists say. All the BOJ can\par really do for now is sit it out and hope that a super-low interest rate\par policy will eventually do its intended job.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One thing tempering the BOJ's sense of crisis is the weakening of the\par yen rate in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. One of the most effective ways to tackle\par deflationary pressure is through a depreciation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and the\par weakening yen is one reason why traders should ignore any rumors of a\par BOJ easing.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Unlike the first half of 1999 when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had to combat a\par strong yen rate through direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, weaker\par economic prospects in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and wobbles in the stock market have caused\par the yen to fall sharply - the yen is now trading around Y118 to the\par dollar, more than 5% cheaper than a month ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With the yen falling against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and long-term interest\par rates sitting at year-and-a-half lows, monetary conditions are already\par easing without the need for further outside interference. That means the\par BOJ can afford to be a little patient.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Market forces have helped the BOJ," said Masaaki Kanno, chief\par economist at JP Morgan Securities Asia and a former BOJ executive\par director.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The weaker yen takes away some of the pain of increasing deflationary\par pressure," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    More Outright JGB Purchases?\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Privately, some BOJ officials support the idea of increasing the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's outright purchases of Japanese government bonds as a\par means of cranking up liquidity.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the moment, the BOJ buys Y200 billion in JGBs outright in a\par twice-monthly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could be increased to\par incorporate purchases of short-term securities should the economy\par continue to falter.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But even if such a step were taken, it would probably have little\par effect on the economy since it would merely add more yen into a market\par that can't find many things to do with its money.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Of course, a rate cut isn't completely out of the question - if stock\par prices continue to nosedive, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would have little choice\par but to reinstate the zero-rate policy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That's because if stocks continue to fall rapidly, that could threaten\par stability in the financial system. Only if it's faced with an imminent\par crisis like that would the BOJ cut rates, Kanno said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That means "that the BOJ's pocket is almost empty," in terms of its\par current monetary easing choices, Kanno said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Nick Shindo, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par nick.shindo@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1042 4131 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx1c0062r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
