\par\par \b \uc2  Yen seen suffering bleak winter. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2000\par 14:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Dec 13 (Reuters) - Sentiment is turning against the yen and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is entering a new, weaker range amid concern Japan's economy could be heading for a tough time. \par \par \uc2  Renewed gloom over prospects for the Japanese economy looked well-founded after a key survey on Wednesday showed corporate confidence stalling and larger manufacturers growing more pessimistic about their business prospects. \par \par \uc2  After holding above 112 against the dollar all year, the so-called tankan survey was in part responsible for a fall that took the yen to 16-month lows beyond 112.50. It is now seen at risk of extending its losses as far as 120 in the coming months. \par \par \uc2  "We have been reasonably cautious on scope for the yen to move ahead even before today's tankan," said Simon Rubinsohn, chief economist at Capel-Cure Sharp in London. \par \par \uc2  "When you look at the current account data and trade flows, some capital is beginning to slip out of the economy." \par \par \uc2  The weaker-than-expected tankan report was seen diminishing the attraction of investing in yen assets at a time when there has been some narrowing in Japan's current account surplus. \par \par \uc2  Data earlier this week showed Japan's current account surplus fell 28.6 percent in October from a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  Signs that domestic investors may also be looking to place money outside Japan came after NTT Docomo said last month it would buy 16 percent of AT&T Wireless for $9.8 billion. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment in Tokyo is getting pretty negative towards the yen," said Michael Lewis, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  The political situation is also making investors nervous, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Yoshiro Mori survived a no confidence vote last month, but is under increasing pressure to resign following a number of verbal gaffes, the resignation of two members of his last cabinet over scandals, and a high-profile rebellion by ruling party reformers. \par \par \uc2  CHARTS BACK UP FUNDAMENTALS \par \par \uc2  The charts mirror the fundamental outlook, technical analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Brian Kiely, chief technical strategist at Royal Bank of Scotland Financial Markets, said the picture looked positive for the dollar against the yen now that it had broken substantially above the 111.75/112 ceiling which had capped it this year. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is shaping up, and should have the strength to get to 118-120 in the next two months, or even quicker than that." \par \par \uc2  The euro lagged the dollar in its gains against the yen on Wednesday, weighed down by a U.S. Supreme Court ruling favouring presidential candidate Republican George W. Bush, seen as likely to be less \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  than rival Democrat Al Gore in support of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But Kiely said the euro would make up ground against the yen in time, clambering above the 100 threshold which has curtailed it since August and stretching as far as 104, highs last reached in March. \par \par \uc2  Any rapid movement in the yen immediately puts \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders on the alert for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity by the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -prone Japanese authorities. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen between April and June, 1998, but turned tail and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least 18 times to curb the yen's strength between January 1999 and April 2000. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said a yen decline as far as 120 to the dollar would leave the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  unfazed, and unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "They will be happy to see it soften," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  "As long as the decline is orderly, it need not become a worry." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwcd0a7pi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
