\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Market Debate Over Lindsey Shift To Yen From Euro \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christine Richard \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2000\par 21:57\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar as political agenda item has been debated for months, with most speculation centering on whether a Bush administration would come to the aid of the euro as the Clinton administration did on Sept. 22. \par \par \uc2  But following comments earlier in December by  Lawrence Lindsey, Republican candidate George W. Bush's chief financial advisor, analysts are asking whether the yen might be the real target of a new administration's dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  Bush remains the market favorite ahead of the outcome of a key Supreme Court ruling set for later Monday. Under a Bush administration, Lindsey is expected to take on the position of chairman at the White House National Economic Council. \par \par \uc2  Following a trip to Japan last month, Lindsey criticized the Clinton administration for eight years of heavy-handed demands that Japan fix its economy through massive fiscal spending. Lindsey said those demands had caused "capital misallocation" as government spending was directed toward low or zero return projects. \par \par \uc2  "Lindsey warned about a forced adjustment in the exchange rate," said Greg Anderson, financial economist at FleetBoston Financial Markets. "We are interpreting that as a Bush presidency will allow a weaker yen." \par \par \uc2  "When Lindsey spoke in Japan he said that the first priority of Japan economic policy should be reducing the budget deficit," said Takanoba Igarashi, chief economist at Sanwa Bank in New York. "He wanted to see instead increased capital investment and consumer spending and I don't think increased exports can be ruled out." \par \par \uc2  "I believe the U.S. could accept larger exports from Japan to the U.S. (under a Bush administration) as long as the government is trying to reduce the debt," said Igarashi. \par \par \uc2  Opening The Floodgates To Imports? \par \par \uc2  But an administration that would encourage Japan to export its way out of crisis seems highly unlikely, according to Marc Chandler, \par \par \uc2  "It would amaze me if a president said we should step up imports," said Chandler. "I'd be flabbergasted." \par \par \uc2  Bush's choice for White House Chief of Staff, Andrew Card, once headed up the now defunct American Automobile Manufacturers Association, which lobbied hard to get Japan to open its markets to U.S. automakers and to ease the trade imbalance, Chandler said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. is expected to run a trade deficit with Japan of around $80 billion this year, with automobiles accounting for a little over 50% of the deficit. \par \par \uc2  The situation with Japan is so serious that it would allow a Bush administration to accept a weaker yen in exchange for a healthier Japanese economy, according to Anderson. \par \par \uc2  "Remember the second most important economy is the world has been sick for ten years," said Anderson. \par \par \uc2  Anderson said a jump in Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in November may reflect a "quiet absorption of dollars from the market (in November) after very modest absorption for last few month." \par \par \uc2  In November, Japan announced that it had accumulated an additional $5.5 billion of reserves. \par \par \uc2  Igarashi agreed that past \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't be ruled out. \par \par \uc2  "Politicians now are accusing the BOJ of raising rates from zero even when the Japanese government had been strongly opposed, so if the economy worsens, the politicians will blame the BOJ," said Igarashi. \par \par \uc2  "If the BOJ and the MOF (\b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) become worried about the near-term economy they will do whatever it takes," Igarashi said. "Silent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a possibility." \par \par \uc2  Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Mellon Financial Corp., said there were other explanations for the jump in reserves. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Some people think there has been quiet \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (to weaken the\par yen) but the ECB (European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) recently confirmed they did a\par swap with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (to allow the ECB to sell yen during an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 )\par and that could explain much of it."\par -By Christine Richard, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4384;\par  christine.richard@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010804dwcb00ttq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
